  predictive or descriptive authority over empirical events. On this reading, the
  world-plan is a regulative rather than constitutive concept, modeled on Kant's use of
  practical postulates.

  RELEVANCE: Offers the main alternative to the constitutive-transcendental reading
  (Nuzzo), and thus defines one pole of the central interpretive debate. James's
  Kantian reading has particular implications for how the "completed sinfulness" of the
  present age functions -- as a diagnosis motivating normative commitment rather than
  as an empirically verified description.

  POSITION: Practical-postulate / Kantian reading; situates the philosophy of history
  within Fichte's political philosophy and moral theory.
  },
  keywords = {philosophy-of-history, five-epochs, practical-reason, postulates, Fichte-Republic, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{landenne2009speculation,
  author  = {Landenne, Quentin},
  title   = {Sp\'eculation et libert\'e dans la philosophie de l'histoire du Caract\`ere de l'\'epoque actuelle de J. G. Fichte (1804--1805)},
  journal = {Revue de m\'etaphysique et de morale},
  year    = {2009},
  volume  = {n\textdegree{} 64},
  number  = {4},
  pages   = {469--487},
  doi     = {10.3917/rmm.094.0143},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Landenne analyzes the intersection of speculative philosophical
  method and the concept of freedom in the Grundzuge, arguing that Fichte's
  philosophy of history is structured by a specific tension between the necessity
  of the speculative world-plan and the freedom that is both its presupposition
  and its telos. The paper examines how freedom functions simultaneously as the
  origin, the content, and the ultimate aim of the historical process.

  RELEVANCE: One of the few French-language analyses specifically devoted to the
  Grundzuge, this article addresses both the teleological structure and the role
  of freedom in the developmental logic -- central to the third key question of
  the research project (how the teleological structure relates the descent from and
  return to reason).

  POSITION: Continental European reading emphasizing the speculative-philosophical
  framework; bridges French reception of Fichte's late philosophy with the
  systematic concerns of the Grundzuge.
  },
  keywords  = {philosophy-of-history, speculation, freedom, teleology, Grundzuge, High},
  abstract = {Cet article propose un commentaire de la philosophie de l ’ histoire développée par Fichte dans ses conférences sur le Caractère de l’époque actuelle (1804-1805), en tentant de reconstruire les différentes étapes de la fondation spéculative duplan universel de l ’ histoire ( Weltplan ) à partir des propositions métaphysiques exposéespopulairement dans la neuvième conférence. La lecture cherche à mettre en évidence lesdéterminations fondamentales du concept de l ’ histoire (réflexivité, factualité, destinationde l ’ humanité) afin de montrer que la liberté du savoir est le principe dernier de lanécessité du Weltplan et de son effectuation.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{breazeale2001pragmatic,
  author  = {Breazeale, Daniel},
  title   = {Fichte's Conception of Philosophy as a ``Pragmatic History of the Human Mind'' and the Contributions of Kant, Platner, and Maimon},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year    = {2001},
  volume  = {62},
  number  = {4},
  pages   = {685--703},
  doi     = {10.1353/jhi.2001.0033},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Breazeale reconstructs Fichte's early concept of a "pragmatic history
  of the human mind" as a philosophical method distinct from both empirical history
  and Kantian critique, tracing its sources in Platner's anthropology, Maimon's
  skeptical challenge, and Kant's transcendental method. He argues that this concept
  shapes the structure of the Wissenschaftslehre and anticipates the later philosophy
  of history.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the intellectual genealogy of Fichte's distinctive approach
  to historical thinking -- in which the philosopher constructs the necessary stages
  of mind's development rather than reporting empirical facts -- that underlies the
  methodology of the Grundzuge.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical reading emphasizing continuity between the Jena
  Wissenschaftslehre and the later philosophy of history; Kant-to-Fichte tradition.
  },
  keywords  = {pragmatic-history, methodology, Kant-Fichte, Wissenschaftslehre, High},
  abstract = {Journal of the History of Ideas - Volume 62, Number 4, October 2001},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{reichl2021firstprinciples,
  author    = {Reichl, Pavel},
  title     = {The Role of First Principles in Fichte's Philosophy of History},
  booktitle = {The Enigma of Fichte's First Principles},
  year      = {2021},
  pages     = {288--308},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi       = {10.1163/9789004459793_015},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reichl examines how Fichte's introduction of a "first principle"
  (Grundsatz) into his philosophy of history attempts to resolve the tension between
  Herder's pluralistic, empirical approach and Kant's a priori chiliasm. He argues
  that while the first-principle strategy innovatively mediates between these
  predecessors, it ultimately fails to secure historiography as a rigorous science,
  because the first principle cannot fully determine the transition from a priori
  structure to empirical content.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the first key question -- how Fichte deduces epochs
  a priori -- by analyzing the foundational methodological move of the Grundzuge and
  its philosophical limitations. Places the Grundzuge in relation to the Herder-Kant
  debate that was its immediate intellectual context.

  POSITION: Critical-constructive reading; positions Fichte between Herder and Kant
  and judges the outcome ambivalent rather than successful.
  },
  keywords  = {first-principles, a-priori-deduction, Herder, Kant-Fichte, High},
  abstract = {In this article, I explore the role of the first principle in Fichte’s philosophy of history and assess the extent to which its introduction is able to resolve problems in the philosophies of history of his predecessors. Particularly, I focus on Fichte’s response to the question of how history can be grasped in a systematic manner for the purposes of theoretical cognition. I argue that while Fichte is able to resolve the tension between Herder’s pluralism and Kant’s chiliasm in an innovative manner, the deployment of his first principle is ultimately unsuccessful in establishing historiography on a firmer scientific foundation.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{picardi2013guiding,
  author    = {Picardi, Roberta},
  title     = {The ``Guiding Thread'' of Universal History: {K}ant's Legacy in Fichte's Philosophy of History},
  booktitle = {Kant und die Philosophie in weltb\"{u}rgerlicher Absicht},
  year      = {2013},
  pages     = {817--830},
  publisher = {DE GRUYTER},
  doi       = {10.1515/9783110246490.4677},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Picardi traces how Fichte's philosophy of history inherits and
  transforms Kant's "guiding thread" of universal history (from the 1784 Idea for
  a Universal History). She argues that Fichte takes over Kant's teleological
  framework -- the idea of a rational purpose immanent in history -- but radicalizes
  it by grounding it not in natural teleology but in the self-positing activity of
  the absolute I, thereby converting the Kantian regulative idea into a constitutive
  principle.

  RELEVANCE: Clarifies the Kant-Fichte relation in philosophy of history and thus
  illuminates what is distinctively Fichtean about the world-plan in the Grundzuge.
  Central to understanding how the a priori deduction differs from Kant's approach.

  POSITION: Comparative Kant-Fichte reading; positions the Grundzuge as a radicalization
  rather than straightforward continuation of Kantian philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {Kant-Fichte, universal-history, guiding-thread, teleology, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{fusaro2016science,
  author  = {Fusaro, Diego},
  title   = {Science of Knowledge and Historical Experience},
  journal = {Semina Scientiarum},
  year    = {2016},
  volume  = {15},
  pages   = {37--63},
  doi     = {10.15633/SS.1767},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fusaro examines key theoretical nexuses of the Grundzuge to show
  how Fichte's Wissenschaftslehre shapes the approach to historical experience in the
  lectures. He argues that the Grundzuge deploys concepts from the Wissenschaftslehre
  (particularly the dialectic of the I and not-I) to structure the historical
  narrative, so that the five epochs are not contingent historical observations but
  systematic derivations from the transcendental structure of self-conscious activity.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a detailed analysis of the relationship between the
  Wissenschaftslehre and the Grundzuge, directly relevant to understanding how the
  a priori deduction of epochs works in practice.

  POSITION: Systematic-idealist reading emphasizing the unity of Fichte's
  transcendental philosophy and his philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords  = {Wissenschaftslehre, historical-experience, Grundzuge, five-epochs, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper sets out as its objective the exploration of some of the key passages and most relevant theoretical plexuses of the Grundzuge des gegenwartigen Zeitalters (1806) by Johann Gottlieb Fichte devoted to the theme of historical narrative and, more generally, of historiography. In particular, we will first of all aim to show the specific status of Historie. Secondly, we will try to outline the differences that Grundzuge establishes between the functions of Wissenschaftslehrer and those of Historiker, in an attempt to show, at the same time, the nexus linking the two and the activities they perform.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{klockonkolowicz2017egoismus,
  author  = {Kloc-Konko{\l}owicz, Jakub},
  title   = {{\guillemotleft}Wir alle werden im Egoismus erzeugt und geboren{\guillemotright}: {F}ichtes \textit{Grundz\"uge des gegenw\"artigen Zeitalters} als selbstreflexive Kritik der Moderne},
  journal = {Fichte und seine Zeit},
  year    = {2017},
  pages   = {234--254},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi     = {10.1163/9789004336674_017},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kloc-Konkolowicz interprets the Grundzuge as a self-reflexive critique
  of modernity rather than merely an external diagnosis of the present age. He argues
  that Fichte identifies the third epoch (of "completed sinfulness" and radical egoism)
  as the Enlightenment's own necessary self-understanding, which contains within itself
  the seeds of its own supersession. The "we" of the title ("we are all generated and
  born in egoism") implicates Fichte himself in the critique.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the second key question -- the meaning and status of
  "completed sinfulness" -- and offers the most developed account of why Fichte treats
  the present age as a necessary and self-undermining stage rather than an accident.

  POSITION: Critical-theory reading; situates the Grundzuge as a philosophy of
  immanent critique of the Enlightenment.
  },
  keywords  = {completed-sinfulness, modernity, egoism, critique-of-Enlightenment, Grundzuge, High},
  abstract = {In the paper I try to interpret Fichte’s Grundzüge des gegenwärtigen Zeitaltersas a reflective self-critique of the Enlightenment. Identifying the ›third historical period‹ of Fichte with the time of Enlightenment, I try to read his lectures as an ambitious and differentiated critique of it. The aim of Fichte’s devastating criticism on dominant characteristics of the Enlightenment (egoism, superficiality and self-interest) is in my opinion not to refuse it entirely. It is rather to continue it with an increased sense of self-criticism. The proposed reading enables to place Fichte’s arguments in the context of today’s debates over issues like the role of religion in civil society, or the meaning and fundamental values of Europe’s integration. For all his critique on modernity, Fichte refrains from treading a conservative, communitarian or collectivist path. This can be seen as the great advantage of his standpoint. What he intends is rather the »enlightenment of Enlightenment«, the continuous differentiation of knowledge and liberating the human being from the constrained form of modern consciousness.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{carvalho2020art,
  author  = {Carvalho, M\'{a}rio Jorge Pereira de Almeida},
  title   = {``The Art of Endlessly Taking Another View''},
  journal = {Revista Filos\'{o}fica de Coimbra},
  year    = {2020},
  volume  = {29},
  number  = {58},
  pages   = {431--482},
  doi     = {10.14195/0872-0851_58_6},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carvalho analyzes the "empty form of knowledge" that Fichte claims
  stands at the center of the third (present) epoch in the Grundzuge, focusing on
  how this empty formalism functions as both a unifying principle for the third epoch
  and a transitional concept toward the fourth. He examines Fichte's claim that a
  single "maxim of comprehensibility" can serve as the common denominator for the
  third and fourth main epochs, and reconstructs the connection to Kant's maxims
  of the self-preservation of reason.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a close reading of the Grundzuge's description of the third
  epoch (the present age), illuminating the second key question about the meaning
  of "completed sinfulness" and the epistemological structure of the present age.

  POSITION: Analytical-textual reading; bridges the epistemological and historical
  dimensions of the Grundzuge's third epoch.
  },
  keywords  = {empty-form-of-knowledge, present-age, third-epoch, Grundzuge, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper deals with Fichte’s The Characteristics of the Present Age, and in particular with his discussion of the “empty form of knowledge” he claims stands at the centre of the third – i.e. the present – age. 
  abstract_source = {s2},
Fichte speaks of a fundamental principle that forms the ‘common denominator’ between the third and fourth main epochs. This fundamental principle – the “maxim of comprehensibility” (Maxime der Begreiflichkeit) – makes knowledge and comprehension the measure of all that “counts as being valid and as really existing”. But the question arises: How can one and the same principle act as the “unifying concept” for two different “main epochs of human life”? Does this not go directly against Fichte’s claim that two main epochs differ from each other in every respect, precisely because they arise from two entirely different “unifying principles”, and because everything in them must reflect the difference between their “unifying principles”? 
Fichte’s answer to this question is as follows:  a)  the fundamental maxim in question allows for two diametrically opposed interpretations, so that each of them provides the principle or the “unifying concept” from which the third and fourth main epochs arise, and b) the third main epoch only gives rise to the empty form of science, as opposed to “truly real science”: it stands for a careless and easy-going, shallow, conventional, trivializing and incorrect conception of the “fundamental maxim of comprehensibility” –  so that it misses what is essential, does not do justice to the fundamental maxim, overlooks its implications, and indeed goes against its innermost meaning. 
Special attention is paid to the question of whether and how some major features of Fichte’s “empty form of knowledge” result from a misguided and superficial understanding of Kant’s “maxims of the self-preservation of reason” and can be reconstructed from this vantage point.},
}

@article{rueda2025correspondences,
  author  = {Rueda Garrido, Daniel},
  title   = {Correspondences Between Fichte's Worldviews and World Ages Based on the Fivefold Synthesis of {WL}-1804-{II}},
  journal = {Synthesis philosophica},
  year    = {2025},
  volume  = {40},
  number  = {2},
  pages   = {205--223},
  doi     = {10.21464/sp40202},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rueda Garrido argues that the logical necessity of both the world-plan
  epochs (as in the Grundzuge) and the typology of worldviews (Weltanschauungen, as in
  the Anweisung zum seligen Leben) can be derived from the fivefold synthesis structure
  of the Wissenschaftslehre 1804-II. He reconstructs the correspondence between the
  five stages of the WL's synthesis and the five historical epochs, showing that
  Fichte's post-Jena systematic works share a common deep structure.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most rigorous recent attempt to show that the five-epoch
  scheme is not merely asserted but systematically derivable from the Wissenschaftslehre,
  directly addressing the key question about how epochs are deduced a priori.

  POSITION: Systematic-reconstruction reading; most recent contribution to the debate
  about the derivability of the five-epoch scheme.
  },
  keywords  = {five-epochs, world-plan, Wissenschaftslehre, WL-1804, systematic-derivation, High},
  abstract = {Na temelju peterostruke sinteze Fichteova Wissenschaftslehre-1804-II ispitujem implicitnu logičku nužnost i svjetsko-planskih epoha i svjetonazora u dvama djelima post-jenskoga razdoblja, u kojima Fichte nastoji objasniti povijest čovječanstva iz a priori plana (Die Grundzüge des gegenwärtigen Zeitalters) te povijest svijesti u njezinu usponu prema višem svjetonazoru (Die Anweisung zum seligen Leben). Svrha je ovoga teksta trostruka: (1) provjeriti odnos izme- đu peterostruke sinteze i svjetonazora; (2) provjeriti odnos između peterostruke sinteze i epoha svjetskoga plana; te (3) provjeriti odnos između točaka 1 i 2. Naravno, postoje ranija istraživanja koja razmatraju oba djela zasebno, no nijedno ne uspostavlja preciznu korelaciju između svjetonazora i svjetsko-planskih epoha. Ovaj članak nastoji popuniti tu prazninu.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{james2015republic,
  author    = {James, David},
  title     = {Fichte's Republic: Idealism, History and Nationalism},
  year      = {2015},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/CBO9781316275719},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James offers a sustained interpretation of Fichte's political and
  historical philosophy, arguing that the Grundzuge and the Addresses to the German
  Nation are best understood as projects that attempt to ground republican political
  ideals in a philosophy of history. The book presents the five epochs as postulates
  of practical reason that motivate political engagement rather than as metaphysical
  necessities, and examines the relationship between the historical schema and
  Fichte's theory of the state, education, and nationalism.

  RELEVANCE: The major Anglophone monograph on Fichte's philosophy of history and
  politics; provides the detailed argumentation behind the practical-postulate reading
  of the five epochs summarized in the 2015 chapter entry above.

  POSITION: Political-philosophy / practical-postulate reading; the book most
  comprehensively representing the Kantian interpretation of the Grundzuge.
  },
  keywords  = {philosophy-of-history, political-philosophy, five-epochs, nationalism, practical-reason, High},
  abstract = {The Addresses to the German Nation (1808) is one of Fichte&amp;apos;s best-known works. It is also his most controversial work because of its nationalist elements. In this book, David James places this text and its nationalism within the context provided by Fichte&amp;apos;s philosophical, educational and moral project of creating a community governed by pure practical reason, in which his own foundational philosophical science or Wissenschaftslehre could achieve general recognition. Rather than marking a break in Fichte&amp;apos;s philosophy, the Addresses to the German Nation and some lesser-known texts from the same period are shown to develop themes already present in his earlier writings. The themes discussed include the opposition between idealism and dogmatism, the role of Fichte&amp;apos;s &amp;apos;popular&amp;apos; lectures and writings in leading individuals to the standpoint of idealism, the view of history demanded by idealism and the role of the state in history.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{elnabolsy2025blyden,
  author  = {El Nabolsy, Zeyad},
  title   = {Edward Wilmot Blyden and Fichte's Nationalist Philosophy of History},
  journal = {Deutsche Zeitschrift f\"ur Philosophie},
  year    = {2025},
  volume  = {73},
  number  = {1},
  pages   = {117--131},
  doi     = {10.1515/dzph-2025-0008},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: El Nabolsy examines the influence of Fichte's philosophy of history
  -- particularly the idea of a Bildungsplan (developmental plan) for humanity
  requiring each people (Volk) to develop its particularity (Eigenthumlichkeit) --
  on Edward Wilmot Blyden's Pan-Africanist philosophy of history. He argues that
  Fichte's notion of racially specific laws of historical development provided a
  framework that Blyden adapted for his defense of a distinctive developmental path
  for peoples of African descent.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the nationalist and particularist dimensions of Fichte's
  philosophy of history, showing how the world-plan doctrine connects to his thinking
  about peoples and nations. Provides a critical perspective on the political
  implications of the Fichtean conception of historical development.

  POSITION: Critical-reception study; connects the Grundzuge's developmental logic
  to the Addresses to the German Nation and its reception history.
  },
  keywords  = {nationalism, Bildungsplan, Volk, philosophy-of-history, reception, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract Edward Wilmot Blyden’s contributions to Pan-Africanism have been widely recognised. Scholars have noted that Blyden’s conception of what he called the “African Personality” reflects the influence of his reading of Herder, Fichte, and Mazzini. However, there has hitherto been no attempt to identify the precise elements that he borrowed from the aforementioned thinkers. This paper focuses on the potential influence of Fichte’s Reden an die deutsche Nation on Blyden’s philosophy of history and his philosophy of education. I argue that while Blyden does not explicitly refer to Fichte, or to Herder for that matter, his philosophy of history as presented in his Islam, Christianity, and the Negro Race, with its emphasis on the existence of racially specific laws of growth, can plausibly be interpreted as having been influenced by Fichte’s philosophy of history. I provide textual and contextual evidence to support this thesis. I show that Fichte’s idea that there is a Bildungsplan for humankind, which requires that each people [Volk] should develop its particularity [Eigenthümlichkeit] provided a suitable framework for Blyden’s defence of a special developmental path for peoples of African descent.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{james2010vocation,
  author  = {James, David},
  title   = {Fichte on the Vocation of the Scholar and the (Mis)use of History},
  journal = {Review of Metaphysics},
  year    = {2010},
  volume  = {63},
  pages   = {539--562},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James examines Fichte's 1794 lectures on the scholar's vocation to
  show how the scholar's role as teacher and guardian of human progress requires both
  a grasp of historical knowledge and the capacity to distinguish between legitimate
  uses of history (as normative guidance) and illegitimate uses (as justification for
  present arrangements). He argues that this early account of history's normative role
  anticipates the fuller philosophy of history of the Grundzuge.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the intellectual-developmental background to the Grundzuge by
  showing how Fichte's engagement with history and its normative function begins in
  his earliest public lectures, establishing the continuity of his thinking about
  history from the Jena period onward.

  POSITION: Intellectual-developmental reading; situates the philosophy of history
  within Fichte's practical philosophy and the role of the scholar.
  },
  keywords = {scholar, vocation, history, normative, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{utteich2020educacion,
  author  = {Utteich, Luciano Carlos},
  title   = {Educaci\'{o}n al rev\'{e}s de la letra en las Lecciones Sexta y S\'{e}ptima de los Grundz\"uge (1806)},
  journal = {Cadernos de Filosofia Alem\~a: Cr\'itica e Modernidade},
  year    = {2020},
  volume  = {25},
  number  = {2},
  pages   = {193--221},
  doi     = {10.11606/issn.2318-9800.v25i2p193-221},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Utteich analyzes Lectures Six and Seven of the Grundzuge -- those
  specifically addressing education -- to show how Fichte conceives education "in
  reverse" (al reves de la letra): in the epoch of completed sinfulness, education
  cannot transmit existing rational principles but must instead work negatively,
  clearing away the obstacles that egoism and empty formalism have erected. The paper
  examines the interval between the present and the future epochs as the practical
  space for educational action.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a close analysis of a specific section of the Grundzuge that is
  relatively neglected in the literature, showing how Fichte's treatment of education
  is structured by his epochal periodization and particularly by the diagnosis of the
  present age as one of "completed sinfulness."

  POSITION: Education-philosophy reading; connects the historical-systematic argument
  to Fichte's practical proposals for pedagogical reform.
  },
  keywords  = {education, Grundzuge, present-age, completed-sinfulness, Low},
  abstract = {Fichte abordó en las Lecciones Sexta y Séptima de Los Caracteres de la Edad Contemporánea [Die Grundzüge des gegenwärtigen Zeitalters, 1806] una importante cuestión relativa al intervalo entre dos períodos o edades, la tercera y la cuarta edad de la humanidad, en su relación de oposición y complementariedad. Para él había llegado el momento de preparar la necesaria transición de la tercera a la cuarta edad de la humanidad, ya que la tercera representaba una falsa autonomía lograda por la letra (por la imprenta) frente al espíritu, siendo éste la verdadera fuente de vivacidad en los asuntos humanos desde su marco inicial, la comunicación oral. En nuestro texto contextualizamos brevemente el tema de los criterios de lectura que presupone la verdadera ciencia de la razón como requisito que apunta a este momento previo en el que la propia letra (discurso escrito o impreso) se genera en la dimensión del espíritu, como la actividad verdaderamente viva que satisface todas las necesidades del pensamiento, ya sea filosófico o literario, en la sociedad.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{james2011fichte,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {Fichte's Social and Political Philosophy: Property and Virtue},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511736131},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Fichte's Foundations of Natural Right and
  Addresses to the German Nation through the dual lenses of property
  theory and civic virtue, arguing that Fichte's political philosophy
  is organized around ensuring that citizens possess adequate property
  as a condition of exercising genuine freedom, and that the state has
  a transformative role in shaping virtuous citizens capable of
  sustaining a rights-based order. James shows how property functions
  in Fichte not as a pre-political natural right but as a politically
  constructed entitlement grounded in intersubjective recognition.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most systematic treatment of Fichte's
  political philosophy as a whole in English, covering both the Jena
  theory of right and the later national writings. It directly
  addresses the question of how political institutions mediate between
  transcendental philosophy and historically realized freedom.

  POSITION: Continuity thesis — the Jena and late Fichte form a
  coherent political philosophy organized around property-grounded
  freedom.
  },
  keywords = {political-philosophy, property, virtue, natural-right, High},
  abstract = {In this study of Fichte's social and political philosophy, David James offers an interpretation of Fichte's most famous writings in this area, including his Foundations of Natural Right and Addresses to the German Nation, centred on two main themes: property and virtue. These themes provide the basis for a discussion of such issues as what it means to guarantee the freedom of all the citizens of a state, the problem of unequal relations of economic dependence between states, and the differences and connections between the legal and political sphere of right and morality. James also relates Fichte's central social and political ideas to those of other important figures in the history of philosophy, including Locke, Kant and Hegel, as well as to the radical phase of the French Revolution. His account will be of importance to all who are interested in Fichte's philosophy and its intellectual and political context},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{nakhimovsky2011closed,
  author = {Nakhimovsky, Isaac},
  title = {The Closed Commercial State: Perpetual Peace and Commercial Society from Rousseau to Fichte},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  doi = {10.1515/9781400838752},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Traces the intellectual genealogy of Fichte's Closed
  Commercial State (1800) from Rousseau's critique of commercial
  society through Kant's project of perpetual peace, arguing that
  Fichte's autarkic state is a coherent, if radical, response to the
  problem of how to make commercial society compatible with republican
  self-governance. Nakhimovsky shows that the Closed Commercial State
  is not an aberration but the logical culmination of a tradition of
  thinking about sovereignty, international trade, and political
  freedom.

  RELEVANCE: Central to understanding how Fichte's philosophy of
  history intersects with his political economy. The book places the
  Closed Commercial State within the broader trajectory from the
  Foundations of Natural Right to the later nationalism, showing how
  economic institutions are conceived as necessary conditions for
  freedom's historical realization.

  POSITION: Situates Fichte in the history of political economy as a
  theorist of the rational state's economic conditions for freedom.
  },
  keywords = {closed-commercial-state, political-economy, perpetual-peace, history, High},
  abstract = {Acknowledgments ix Introduction 1 Chapter 1: Perpetual Peace and Fichte's Theory of the State 15 Herder's Letter 15 Perpetual Peace and Power Politics 17 The Citizen of Frejus and the Philosopher of Konigsberg 22 The Citizen of Frejus, the Philosopher of Konigsberg, and the Professor at Jena 35 Toward The Closed Commercial State 61 Chapter 2: Commerce and the European Commonwealth in 1800 63 Gentz's Review 63 Perpetual Peace and The Closed Commercial State 65 Fichte's History of Commerce 74 Prussia and the Anglo-French Debate of 1800 84 Fichte's Contribution to the Debate 98 Chapter 3: R epublicanization in Theory and Practice 103 Fichte's Proposal 103 Fichte's Implementation Strategy 106 The Closed Commercial State and the Political Economy of Prussian Reform 115 Fichte's Moral Challenge Continued 126 Chapter 4: Fichte's Political Economy of the General Will 130 Hestermann's Review 130 Open Commercial State versus Closed Commercial State 134 Needs and Rights in Fichte's Theory of Property 143 The Transcendental Industrialism of The Closed Commercial State 157 Conclusion 166 Bibliography 177 Index 195},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{ware2020fichte,
  author = {Ware, Owen},
  title = {Fichte's Moral Philosophy},
  year = {2020},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/oso/9780190086596.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Offers a sustained reading of Fichte's System of
  Ethics (1798) as an "ethics of wholeness," arguing that Fichte's
  ethics is structured around a theory of drives (Triebe) that unites
  freedom, moral feeling, and conscience. Challenges the standard
  reading that Fichte's ethics is simply a radicalization of Kantian
  autonomy by showing the role of affective and communal elements.

  RELEVANCE: The System of Ethics is one of Fichte's three major
  practical works alongside the Foundations of Natural Right and the
  Closed Commercial State; this study provides the authoritative
  English-language treatment of its arguments, essential for
  understanding how ethics relates to right and history in Fichte's
  practical system.

  POSITION: Interprets ethics as complementing rather than grounding
  right, contributing to discussions of how freedom's practical
  dimensions relate to its historical realization.
  },
  keywords = {ethics, moral-philosophy, System-of-Ethics, freedom, High},
  abstract = {This book develops and defends a new interpretation of Fichte’s moral philosophy as an ethics of wholeness. While virtually forgotten for most of the twentieth century, Fichte’s System of Ethics (1798) is now recognized by scholars as a masterpiece in the history of post-Kantian philosophy and a key text for understanding the work of later German idealist thinkers. This book provides a careful examination of the intellectual context in which Fichte’s moral philosophy evolved and of the specific arguments he offers in response to Kant and his immediate successors. A distinctive feature of the study is a focus on the foundational concepts of Fichte’s ethics—freedom, morality, feeling, conscience, community—and their connection to his novel but largely misunderstood theory of drives. By way of conclusion, the book shows that what appears to be two conflicting commitments in Fichte’s ethics, a commitment to the feelings of one’s conscience and a commitment to engage in open dialogue with others, are two aspects of his theory of moral perfection. The result is a fresh understanding of Fichte’s System of Ethics as offering a compelling resolution to the personal and interpersonal dimensions of moral life.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{nance2012recognition,
  author = {Nance, Michael},
  title = {Recognition, Freedom, and the Self in {Fichte}'s {Foundations of Natural Right}},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {23},
  number = {3},
  pages = {608--632},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1468-0378.2012.00552.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Presents a detailed reconstruction of Fichte's
  transcendental argument in the Foundations of Natural Right for
  mutual recognition as a condition of the possibility of
  self-consciousness. Identifies what Nance calls a "modal dilemma":
  either the necessity Fichte establishes is too strong (metaphysically
  implausible) or too weak (failing to ground normative conclusions
  about right). Proposes a reading that escapes this dilemma by
  distinguishing the argument's transcendental structure from its
  normative implications.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the foundational structure of the
  Foundations of Natural Right and the relationship between recognition
  and freedom — two concepts central to the domain's question of how
  right is grounded in intersubjective conditions that history must
  actualize.

  POSITION: Recognition-focused analytical reading of the FNR, in
  debate with McNulty (2016) over the modal structure of Fichte's
  argument.
  },
  keywords = {recognition, Anerkennung, natural-right, intersubjectivity, transcendental, High},
  abstract = {In this paper I present an interpretation of J. G. Fichte's transcendental argument for the necessity of mutual recognition (Anerkennung) in Foundations of Natural Right. Fichte's argument purports to show that, as a condition of the possibility of self-consciousness, we must take ourselves to stand in relations of mutual recognition with other agents like ourselves. After reconstructing the steps of Fichte's argument, I present what I call the ‘modal dilemma’, which highlights a serious ambiguity in Fichte's deduction. According to the modal dilemma, the conclusion to Fichte's transcendental argument—that as a condition of the possibility of our self-consciousness, we must recognize and be recognized by others—expresses either metaphysical or normative necessity. However, no normative conclusion follows from Fichte's premises, and the metaphysical claim that does follow from his argument appears to be implausibly strong. Thus the argument looks like a failure on either interpretation of the conclusion's modality. In the penultimate section of the paper, I propose a new interpretation of the argument that avoids the modal dilemma and provides a normative grounding of Fichte's concept of right.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mcnulty2016transcendental,
  author = {McNulty, Jacob},
  title = {Transcendental Philosophy and Intersubjectivity: Mutual Recognition as a Condition for the Possibility of Self-Consciousness in Sections 1--3 of {Fichte}'s {Foundations of Natural Right}},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {24},
  number = {4},
  pages = {788--810},
  doi = {10.1111/ejop.12131},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Defends a new interpretation of Fichte's argument for
  mutual recognition in the opening sections of the Foundations of
  Natural Right, addressing what Nance calls the modal dilemma. McNulty
  argues that the argument relies on a distinctive kind of practical
  necessity: recognizing others is necessary not as a logical condition
  but as a condition of the rational activity of self-positing.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the most sophisticated recent account of the
  transcendental argument connecting self-consciousness, freedom, and
  recognition — the philosophical core of how Fichte grounds right
  independently of moral theory and positions it as a structure
  requiring historical realization.

  POSITION: Recognition-focused, in dialogue with Nance (2012); defends
  a distinctive modal reading that has implications for the normative
  status of right.
  },
  keywords = {recognition, intersubjectivity, transcendental, natural-right, self-consciousness, High},
  abstract = {Abstract In the opening sections of his Foundations of Natural Right , Fichte argues that mutual recognition is a condition for the possibility of self‐consciousness. However, the argument turns on the apparently unconvincing claim that, in the context of transcendental philosophy, conceptions of the subject as an isolated individual give rise to a vicious circle the resolution of which requires the introduction of a second rational being to ‘summon’ the first. In this essay, my aim is to present a revised account of the opening arguments on which they are more convincing. In particular, I argue that the problem of a circle is genuine and may be seen to result from a relation of mutual dependence between agency and cognition which ensures that for an exercise of either capacity to take place, an exercise of the other would have already had to have taken place with the result that neither can occur. Moreover, the solution is successful. The summons (the claim of the other) prevents us from being driven around the circle once more because it is a ‘synthesis’ that reconciles the constraint to which I am subject as a cognizer of independently given objects and my freedom as a self‐determining subject.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{arreseigror2019morality,
  author = {Arrese Igor, Hector Oscar},
  title = {Morality and State in the {Fichtean} Political Philosophy},
  journal = {Araucaria},
  year = {2019},
  number = {41},
  pages = {79--96},
  doi = {10.12795/araucaria.2019.i41.04},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Fichte's philosophy of history in the
  Characteristics of the Present Age (1804/5) is required to resolve
  a fundamental problem in the synchronic Jena theory of right: the
  Foundations of Natural Right cannot guarantee the stability of a
  state composed solely of rationally self-interested individuals,
  because its mechanism of coercive enforcement (the ephorate) requires
  a degree of civic virtue inconsistent with the system's own
  foundational assumptions. The diachronic perspective supplied by the
  philosophy of history enables Fichte to project the resolution of
  this inconsistency into a future epoch of genuine civic solidarity.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most direct treatment of how Fichte's
  philosophy of history is required by the internal structure of his
  political philosophy — exactly the relationship at the center of the
  research project's key questions.

  POSITION: Rupture/tension thesis — the philosophy of history is
  needed to address problems Fichte cannot solve at the synchronic
  level of right.
  },
  keywords = {philosophy-of-history, natural-right, state, morality, continuity, High},
  abstract = {The philosophy of history of 1804 and 1805 enables Fichte to place his natural right, developed previously at Jena, against a diachronic background. This means that Fichte does not reason merely synchronically from a timeless conception of society and state. From a synchronic viewpoint, Fichte cannot solve the problem of the control of political power because he has to draw on the assumption of a virtuous ephorate. This assumption is not consistent with the Fichtean ideal of a philosophy of right completely independent from moral considerations. Thus, the control of government is possible only if at least a group of citizens can go beyond the mere rational egoism. This new temporal conception of the state leads Fichte to think that the problems of consistency of his theory of Jena are unavoidable, given that a society integrated by egoist individuals cannot be sustained. However, his later philosophy of history enables Fichte to state the inexorable annihilation of this type of community and gives place to an ensuing epoch, when citizens are not self-interested anymore.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{james2016staatslehre,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {The Political Theology of {Fichte}'s {Staatslehre}: Immanence and Transcendence},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {24},
  number = {6},
  pages = {1157--1175},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2016.1207611},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the late Staatslehre (1813) and its attempt
  to provide a normative-political theory in which the state is charged
  with realizing moral freedom historically. James argues that Fichte
  introduces a concept of transcendence (via the figure of the
  Zwingherr — the "compellor") that implicitly borrows from political
  theology, creating a tension between the immanent logic of right and
  the transcendent necessity of a quasi-providential political
  authority to force historical actualization.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the late period of Fichte's political
  philosophy, where the state takes on a stronger historical-teleological
  role. Illuminates the development from the early Jacobin Fichte
  through the Addresses to the German Nation to the authoritarian
  tendencies of the Staatslehre.

  POSITION: Continuity thesis with qualifications — the late Fichte
  is read as extending the Jena project while introducing new tensions.
  },
  keywords = {Staatslehre, late-fichte, political-theology, state, freedom, High},
  abstract = {Given its use of religious concepts and language, it is tempting to class Fichte’s rarely discussed Staatslehre as a political theology. I argue that the Staatslehre can be classed as a political theology because of the way in which it can be understood in terms of the concepts of immanence and transcendence. The concept of immanence applies to Fichte’s account of history in particular. Fichte himself allows for a moment of transcendence at the very beginning of history. I argue that the concept of transcendence is also implicit in the Staatslehre in relation to some problems faced by Fichte’s own account of the role of the Zwingherr in the historical development and actualization of right and moral freedom, despite his attempt to avoid introducing theologically based explanations of political concepts at this stage of his Staatslehre.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{maliks2025politics,
  author = {Maliks, Reidar},
  title = {Fichte's Politics of Cultural Awakening},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {30},
  number = {1},
  pages = {29--43},
  doi = {10.1017/s1369415425000159},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Fichte's political philosophy across the
  early-to-late trajectory is unified by a theory of "cultural
  awakening": the state's task is not merely to enforce right but to
  transform citizens into genuinely self-determining rational agents
  capable of sustaining republican institutions. Maliks reads this as
  a coherent extension of the Jena project rather than a nationalist
  departure, connecting the Addresses to the German Nation and the
  Staatslehre back to the Foundations of Natural Right.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the key research question about the
  relationship between the early republican Fichte and the later
  statist/national Fichte. Provides an integrative account that
  challenges the standard rupture narrative.

  POSITION: Continuity thesis — the politics of cultural awakening
  unifies Fichte's practical philosophy from 1793 to 1814.
  },
  keywords = {cultural-awakening, nationalism, republicanism, state, late-fichte, High},
  abstract = {Abstract Although scholars agree that Fichte’s earliest political writings are Kantian, they contain a theory of individual emancipation through a culture of perfection that is foreign to Kant’s Doctrine of Right. I argue that Fichte based his theory on Kant’s moral duty and therefore derived the conclusion that individual morality should be the constitution’s aim. As a result, principles of right are not limited to securing relations of external freedom among equals but concerned with creating a society of autonomous individuals. Reaching that end goes through emancipation both from the oppression of sensibility over rationality and from the false consciousness that upholds voluntary servitude to unjust regimes. As an alternative Kantian path, Fichte provides philosophical grounding for movements seeking political emancipation through cultural awakening.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{clarke2011hegel,
  author = {Clarke, James},
  title = {Hegel's Critique of {Fichte} in the 1802/3 Essay on Natural Right},
  journal = {Inquiry},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {54},
  number = {3},
  pages = {207--225},
  doi = {10.1080/0020174x.2011.574998},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Hegel's critique of Fichte's Foundations
  of Natural Right in the 1802/3 essay on natural right, arguing that
  Hegel's objection is that Fichte's intersubjective theory of right
  remains "abstract" — it cannot generate genuine ethical life (Sittlichkeit)
  because it treats recognition purely as a formal mechanism rather than
  as constitutive of a concrete community. Clarke evaluates the critique
  as raising a genuine problem for Fichte even if not fully vindicating
  Hegel's alternative.

  RELEVANCE: Clarifies the philosophical stakes in the transition from
  Fichte's theory of right to later German idealist philosophy of the
  state; places Fichte's account of right and recognition in a critical
  philosophical dialogue that illuminates its limits.

  POSITION: Critical of the Jena theory's abstractness from a Hegelian
  standpoint; important interlocutor for the Fichte-Hegel transition.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, natural-right, recognition, critique, ethical-life, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract This paper provides a reconstruction and critical assessment of Hegel's critique of Fichte's political philosophy in his 1802/3 essay On the Scientific Ways of Treating Natural Right. I argue that Hegel's critique, while not entirely successful, raises a serious problem for Fichte's political philosophy as presented in the 1796/7 Foundations of Natural Right.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{clarke2009fichte,
  author = {Clarke, James Alexander},
  title = {Fichte and {Hegel} on Recognition},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {17},
  number = {2},
  pages = {365--385},
  doi = {10.1080/09608780902761745},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Contrasts Fichte's account of recognition in the
  Foundations of Natural Right with Hegel's in the 1802/3 System of
  Ethical Life. Argues that Fichte's recognition is instrumental and
  normatively thin — it is a mutual acknowledgment of rights without
  moral content — whereas Hegel demands that recognition also constitute
  an attitude of moral regard. Clarke defends the significance of this
  difference for the philosophy of right and ethical life.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a careful comparison of Fichte and Hegel on
  recognition that situates Fichte's theory in the tradition and
  clarifies what is at stake in the move from right to ethical life.

  POSITION: Fichte-Hegel comparative framework, emphasizing the
  distinction between legal and moral recognition.
  },
  keywords = {recognition, Hegel, right, ethical-life, intersubjectivity, Medium},
  abstract = {In this paper I provide an interpretation of Hegel’s account of ‘recognition’ (Anerkennung) in the 1802-3 System of Ethical Life as a critique of Fichte’s account of recognition in the 1796-7 Foundations of Natural Right. In the first three sections of the paper I argue that Fichte’s account of recognition in the domain of right is not concerned with recognition as a moral attitude. I then turn, in section four, to a discussion of Hegel’s critique and transformation of Fichte’s conception of recognition. Hegel’s transformation consists, I argue, in the claim that a comprehensive account of recognition in the domain of right must be concerned with recognition as a moral attitude.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{nance2019property,
  author = {Nance, Michael},
  title = {Property and Economic Planning in {Fichte}'s Contractualism},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {27},
  number = {3},
  pages = {643--660},
  doi = {10.1111/ejop.12446},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Fichte's theory of property across the
  Foundations of Natural Right and the Closed Commercial State, arguing
  that Fichte's republicanism commits him to rejecting both classical
  liberal property rights and a market economy. The state is required
  to enforce an economic plan as a condition of ensuring that every
  citizen has guaranteed access to the property needed for free agency.

  RELEVANCE: Links the theory of right in the FNR to the political
  economy of the Closed Commercial State, showing how the normative
  foundations of Fichte's state commit it to economic planning as a
  condition for freedom's realization.

  POSITION: Property-focused reading of Fichte's contractualism,
  emphasizing the statist implications of the theory of right.
  },
  keywords = {property, political-economy, closed-commercial-state, contractualism, state, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract My paper reconstructs Fichte's property theory and political economy in Foundations of Natural Right and The Closed Commercial State . Fichte's theory of property requires the rejection of the classical liberal theory of property rights. Fichte's alternative theory of property, in conjunction with his republican account of the state's role in guaranteeing individual rights, further requires the rejection of a market economy in favor of a planned economy. For Fichte's view entails the normative necessity of a political economy in which the production and transfer of goods and services, across large sectors of the economy, are mandated by the state in advance in accordance with an economic plan. As a result, Fichte reconceives his contractualism as necessarily including the negotiation of a state‐enforced plan for economic activity. Thus, Fichte's new theory of property in Foundations of Natural Right has extremely wide‐ranging implications for the rest of his political and economic thought. The negotiation of fair terms of political association characteristic of classical contractualist thought becomes in Fichte's hands the negotiation of an economic plan that defines each individual's socioeconomic rights. I conclude with some brief remarks on the sense in which Fichte's theory serves as a cautionary tale for later socialists.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{alessiato2021striving,
  author = {Alessiato, Elena Paola Carola},
  title = {Striving for Freedom. Some Notes about {Fichte}'s Idealism},
  journal = {Ethics in Progress},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {12},
  number = {2},
  pages = {46--60},
  doi = {10.14746/eip.2021.2.4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes freedom in Fichte's idealism across
  transcendental, moral, and social-historical registers. Starting from
  the Tathandlung, traces how freedom as infinite striving (Streben)
  connects the I's transcendental self-positing with moral activity in
  the world and with the social goal of a free community. Argues for
  the unity of Fichte's system as a philosophy in which individual
  freedom and the historical goal of rational community are internally
  related.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a synoptic account of freedom's multiple
  dimensions in Fichte — from the foundational Wissenschaftslehre to
  ethics and social philosophy — connecting the theoretical and
  practical aspects that bear on the philosophy of history question.

  POSITION: Unitary-system reading that connects Fichte's
  transcendental idealism to its practical and historical dimensions.
  },
  keywords = {freedom, striving, idealism, Tathandlung, moral-philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {Moving from Fichte’s assumption that “the essence of the I is its activity”, this paper tries to analyze the meaning and implications of the idea of “activity” [Tathandlung] in order to explicate the peculiarities of Fichte’s critical, transcendental, and moral idealism. Fichte’s idea of activity will be examined with reference to such basic concepts as collision [Anstoss], interaction [Wechselwirkung], inter-determination [Wechselbestimmung], and striving [Streben]. However, it is freedom which frames and connects the core components of Fichte’s thinking and sets up the goal of his philosophy of action. What freedom accounts for, can be identified both at the transcendental level, in the internal dynamic of infinity and finitude constituting the subjectivity of the I, and at the moral and social levels of Fichte’s thought, as the goal of the human action in history and in the society. In assuming the unitary character of Fichte’s philosophical system, concluding remarks are developed concerning the moral meaning of the act of striving for freedom and, conversely, the immorality of attitudes and feelings such as fear, resignation, and fatigue.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{james2010reappraisal,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {Fichte's Reappraisal of {Kant}'s Theory of Cosmopolitan Right},
  journal = {History of European Ideas},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {36},
  number = {1},
  pages = {61--70},
  doi = {10.1016/j.histeuroideas.2009.07.003},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Fichte's Closed Commercial State is best
  understood as a critique and revision of Kant's cosmopolitan right.
  Whereas Kant permits commercial interaction among states as a form of
  cosmopolitan hospitality, Fichte argues that asymmetric economic
  dependence generated by free trade undermines genuine political
  independence. The closed commercial state is therefore Fichte's
  answer to the problem of how to preserve state sovereignty as a
  condition of freedom in an interconnected world.

  RELEVANCE: Clarifies the political-philosophical motivation for the
  Closed Commercial State by situating it in relation to Kant's
  international political philosophy, revealing how economic autarchy
  is a consequence of Fichte's commitment to right-based freedom.

  POSITION: Places the Closed Commercial State in Kant-Fichte
  continuity/contrast framework regarding cosmopolitan right.
  },
  keywords = {closed-commercial-state, Kant, cosmopolitan-right, international, sovereignty, Medium},
  abstract = {I argue that although in the Foundations of Natural Right Fichte adopts a theory of cosmopolitan right that is in a number of important respects formally identical to the one developed by Kant, he later came in The Closed Commercial State to reassess his earlier Kantian cosmopolitanism. This work can in fact be seen to identify a problem with Kant's cosmopolitanism, namely, Kant's failure to recognize the possibility of an indirect form of coercion based on unequal relations of economic dependence. I argue that Kant's failure to acknowledge such a possibility stems from his uncritical acceptance of this type of relation. Whereas Fichte's awareness of the possibility of one-sided forms of economic dependence leads him to offer a solution to the problems it raises: the severing of all commercial relations with other states. This solution is itself highly problematic, however, as I indicate with reference to Fichte's remarks on ‘natural frontiers’.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{ramsauer2023thinking,
  author = {Ramsauer, Laurenz},
  title = {Between Thinking and Acting: {Fichte}'s Deduction of the Concept of Right},
  journal = {Manuscrito},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {46},
  number = {2},
  pages = {156--197},
  doi = {10.1590/0100-6045.2023.v46n2.lr},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Provides a careful reconstruction of Fichte's
  a priori deduction of the concept of right in the Foundations of
  Natural Right, emphasizing the move from pure self-positing activity
  to the practical necessity of limiting one's freedom in light of
  others. Argues that Fichte's deduction generates a genuinely
  non-moral normativity of right — obligation arises from the
  conditions of agency itself, not from the moral law.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the philosophical foundations of Fichte's
  theory of right as an independent systematic domain, clarifying what
  kind of normativity is at stake when freedom is institutionalized
  through legal and political structures in history.

  POSITION: Analytical reconstruction of the FNR's deductive
  structure, arguing for right's independence from morality.
  },
  keywords = {natural-right, deduction, normativity, agency, right, Medium},
  abstract = {: Fichte’s ambitious project in the Foundations of Natural Right is to provide an a priori deduction of the concept of right independently from morality. So far, interpretations of Fichte’s deduction of the concept of right have persistently fallen into one of two rough categories: either they (re)interpret the normative necessity of right in terms of moral or quasi-moral normativity or they interpret right’s normative necessity in terms of hypothetical imperatives. However, each of these interpretations faces significant exegetical difficulties. By contrast, I argue that we can  I am grateful to the participants of the University of Chicago’s German Philosophy Workshop for their helpful feedback on an earlier draft of this paper. Special thanks are owed to Matthias Haase, whose insightful feedback has helped me immensely thinking through this topic.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{bradley2025incomplete,
  author = {Bradley, J. Colin},
  title = {Fichte's Incomplete Republicanism},
  booktitle = {Kant's Early Followers in Political Philosophy},
  year = {2025},
  pages = {47--68},
  doi = {10.4324/9781003516545-4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Characterizes Fichte's political philosophy as an
  "incomplete republicanism" — a theory that commits to republican
  principles of collective self-governance and the rule of law but
  cannot fully realize them within its own theoretical resources.
  Bradley traces the incompleteness to the structural tension between
  the theory of right's formalism and the concrete institutional
  demands of self-governing republics, which the philosophy of history
  is intended to address.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the developmental question of how
  Fichte's early republican commitments relate to his later statist
  views, framing the trajectory as an attempt (never fully achieved)
  to complete a republican political philosophy.

  POSITION: Tension thesis with continuity elements — republicanism
  is incomplete but remains the animating goal throughout.
  },
  keywords = {republicanism, political-philosophy, right, state, development, Medium},
  abstract = {Despite obvious similarities, Kant's and Fichte's philosophies of right contain sharply different understandings of the inadequacy of the state of nature and the basic justification for a coercive state. Both argue that rights in a state of nature are “provisional” or “incomplete” and that a coercive republic is necessary to guarantee freedom for people who cannot help interacting with one another. But for Fichte, the problem with the state of nature is that we cannot trust one another to protect our rights, whereas for Kant the problem is that no private person has the authority to enforce her rights over others. This chapter argues that Fichte's focus on trust prevents him from developing a consistently republican position that the state and rule of law really are necessary for freedom. Fichte is unable to explain why coercion is a necessary part of right, and so, from Kant's perspective, Fichte's Foundations of Natural Right fails to constitute a philosophy of right at all.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{williams1992recognition,
