  },
  keywords = {fichte, language, urvolk, supersensuous, embodiment, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{iqbal2026embodied,
  author = {Iqbal, Zafar},
  title = {Embodied Universality: {Fichte}, {Wal\={i}ull\={a}h}, and the Paradox of Universal Norms},
  journal = {Open Philosophy},
  year = {2026},
  volume = {9},
  number = {1},
  doi = {10.1515/opphil-2025-0102},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Iqbal compares Fichte's account of how a particular
  people (the German Urvolk) embodies universal norms with the parallel
  structure in the thought of Shah Walīullāh of Delhi, where Islam's
  universal law is to be realized through a particular community. He argues
  that both thinkers face the same paradox: how can a universal normative
  order be necessarily mediated by a particular language, culture, or
  religious community? Iqbal uses this comparison to expose the internal
  tensions in Fichte's philosophy of history.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the philosophical structure of the relationship
  between universalism and particularism in Fichte's historical teleology —
  central to the project — through cross-cultural comparison that helps
  distinguish structural features from contingent German-Romantic ones.

  POSITION: Comparative philosophy; recent.
  },
  keywords = {fichte, universalism, particularism, urvolk, nationalism, comparative-philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 
 This paper attempts to explore the question of universality and historical contingency in the philosophy of Johann Gottlieb Fichte with special regard to the performative dimensions of the
 Wissenschaftslehre
 and the Addresses to the
 German Nation
 (1807–08). It argues that Fichte’s
 Aufforderung
 (summons) in the Foundations of Natural Right (1796–97) attempts to establish intersubjectivity in normative aspiration to universal ethics, and its use in public pedagogy is, however, fraught with the danger of national particularism in the construction of recognition. This is part of the reason that Fichte is discussed together with Shāh Walīullāh al-Dehlawī’s
 Ḥujjat
 Allāh
 al-Bāligha
 and
 Al-Budūr al-Bāzigha,
 which derive universality from the unity of God (tawḥīd) and the aims of God’s law (
 maqāṣid al-sharīʿa
 ) as expressed in the institutions of
 Zakāt
 ,
 ḥisbah
 , and
 khilāfah
 . The study finds asymmetrical vulnerabilities in the cases: Fichte’s subjective performance is open to particularism, and in the case of Walīullāh, the juridical model suffers from the lack of interpretative contingency of the Mughal period. Hence, the major conclusions of the study are: Fichte should be recast and the nationalist readings stripped away, then the Habermasian discourse ethics and decolonial critiques and post-Kantian comparative philosophy can be advanced with Fichte’s rethinking of universal ethics through contextual mediations. The paper fosters the dialogue on the restrictions of idealism and the qualifications of the risks to universality on both systems.
},
}

@article{scribner2017review,
  author = {Scribner, F. Scott},
  title = {Review: {Fichte}'s {Addresses} to the {German Nation} Reconsidered, ed. by {Daniel Breazeale} and {Tom Rockmore}},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {55},
  number = {3},
  pages = {548--549},
  doi = {10.1353/hph.2017.0062},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Scribner's review assesses the Breazeale-Rockmore volume,
  noting that few philosophical texts have suffered as much from their
  reception history as the Addresses — alternately overestimated as a
  founding document of modern nationalism and underestimated as merely a
  piece of Napoleonic-era rhetoric. He identifies the volume's key
  contribution as restoring the philosophical seriousness of the text while
  acknowledging the legitimate concerns raised by its ideological afterlife.

  RELEVANCE: Useful for situating the Breazeale-Rockmore volume within the
  broader interpretive debate; Scribner's framing of the interpretive stakes
  is directly relevant to the project's account of how scholarship has
  approached Fichte's legacy.

  POSITION: Critical survey; acknowledges both systematic-philosophical and
  reception-critical perspectives.
  },
  keywords = {fichte, addresses-german-nation, reception, nationalism, Low},
  abstract = {Reviewed by: Fichte's Addresses to the German Nation Reconsidered ed. by Daniel Breazeale and Tom Rockmore F. Scott Scribner Daniel Breazeale and Tom Rockmore, editors. Fichte's Addresses to the German Nation Reconsidered. Albany: State University of New York Press, 2016. Pp. 312. Cloth, $90.00. Interpretation always takes place in the present tense. It is worth reminding ourselves of this, because few philosophical texts or treatises have suffered the rise and fall of the vagaries of their own contemporary Weltanschauung as Fichte's Addresses to the German Nation. Few texts in history have been simultaneously so overestimated and underestimated in their impact and importance as Fichte's Addresses; and therefore few texts can be said to be so misunderstood—and so need in of reassessment. This collection, Fichte's Addresses to the German Nation Reconsidered, edited by Daniel Breazeale and Tom Rockmore, precisely seeks to fill this lacuna. The interpretative fate of the Addresses has always hinged on the watchword of 'nationalism' and the time-horizon that shapes a given interpreter's view of it. For instance, it is clear that a cry for national unity at a time when Germany had not yet achieved nationhood, and was suffering from foreign occupation, has a far different ring to it than a cry for nationalism in the shadow of the Third Reich. And, although this collection of essays was composed well before our own witness of the current worldwide resurgence of nationalism—in the form of Brexit, the U.S. election of Donald J. Trump, and the general rise of nationalism across much of Europe—it is nevertheless important to work to remain self-conscious of our own interpretive horizon. This collection begins, as it should, with an excellent, well-researched, historical introduction to Fichte's Addresses. Here, Breazeale rightly thematizes the erratic trajectory of the Addresses reception. They were published in 1808, just months before the end of Napoleon's French occupation. They are based on a series of "Sunday lectures" given by Fichte in 1806. Their reception has indeed been uneven. If the initial lectures were charged with a rebellious excitement—especially given that a local bookseller had recently been executed by Napoleon for treasonous pamphlets—the Addresses were, in turn, given little to no credit by state representatives at the official constitution of German nationhood. And this ebb and flow is repeated yet again. And again. If Fichte's obituary acknowledges him as a "profound thinker," with the "greatest service to the fatherland" (10), in the twentieth century, following the rise of Nazism, the fortunes of the Addresses are again reversed. And, as a consequence, Breazeale rightly describes the fate of their reception since World War II as "toxic material" (12). And it is precisely the task of this collection, then, to reassess the possible hyperbole of this "toxic" label and to judiciously reconsider the value of Fichte's Addresses. The collection of thirteen essays embraces the full range of material in Fichte's Addresses—covering not only the topic of nationalism, but also culture, language, education, ethics, and, of course, the science of philosophy or Wissenschaft. Not surprisingly, the bulk of these essays confront, in various guises, Fichte and the question of nationalism. A key problem for [End Page 548] interpreters has long been to establish an interpretative rapprochement between Fichte's earlier, Kantianinspired sense of freedom and liberal values, and his later turn toward more authoritarian leanings. Breazeale, Rainer Schäfer, Gabriel Gottlieb and Rockmore all work towards resolving this seeming conflict between the earlier and later Fichte in various ways, while Marina F. Bykova and Arnold Farr consider Fichte's unique approach to nationalism more directly. Most essays display scholarly rigor and innovation in their approach. The erratic historical reception of Fichte's Addresses as mere nationalist fodder is, in large part, caused by peoples' failure to actually read them. And, as many essays here show, the Addresses offer insight into a wide range of philosophic concerns. A number of essays here explore Fichte's philosophy language, education, and culture. For example, Bykova, rejects the nationalist reading outright, in favor a practical-moral reading; Sila Özkara shows the conflict between Fichte's theory...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{dimic2021pestalozzi,
  author = {Dimi\'{c}, Zoran},
  title = {{Pestalozzi} and {Fichte}: The Power of Education},
  journal = {Godišnjak za pedagogiju},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {6},
  number = {2},
  pages = {71--81},
  doi = {10.46630/gped.2.2021.5},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dimic examines the relationship between Pestalozzi's
  educational theory and Fichte's program of Nationalerziehung in the
  Addresses, arguing that Fichte drew on Pestalozzi's insight that education
  must engage the whole person — will, feeling, and intellect — rather than
  merely transmitting knowledge. Fichte transformed this pedagogical principle
  into a national-political project: the regeneration of Germany through
  education that instills love of the fatherland at a pre-reflective level.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses the educational dimension of the Addresses, tracing
  the Pestalozzian sources of Nationalerziehung and showing how the
  philosophy of education connects to the broader philosophy of history.

  POSITION: History of pedagogy; situates Fichte in the context of
  Enlightenment and Romantic educational reform.
  },
  keywords = {fichte, national-education, pestalozzi, nationalerziehung, Low},
  abstract = {The text explores the relationship between Pestalozzi and Fichte. The author argues that, with the exception of Kant, Pestalozzi had probably the strongest influence on the development of Fichte’s philosophy, and especially on his placing education in the focus of his philosophy. The influence of Pestalozzi on Fichte is most visible in Fichte’s critique of the existing education and the construction of a completely new conception of educational activity. It is at this point that Fichte directly relies on Pestalozzi’s insights into the importance of independence and self-activity in pedagogical practice. At the same time, there are significant differences between the two of them, clearly visible in Fichte’s conception of national education. This conception of Fichte must by no means be viewed only from the context of The Addresses to the German Nation but must be viewed as a whole of the development of Fichte’s philosophy, and especially in relation to the historical and educational circumstances that prevailed at the time. Keywords: education, independence, self-activity, selfishness, nation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{perovich2006mysticism,
  author = {Perovich Jr., Anthony N.},
  title = {On the Mysticism of {F}ichte's {The Way Towards the Blessed Life}},
  journal = {Idealistic Studies},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {36},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--11},
  doi = {10.5840/idstudies20063619},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Perovich argues that Fichte's 1806 lectures constitute a
  genuine form of mysticism -- specifically, a mysticism of intellectual
  intuition of the divine -- rather than a mere ethical piety or
  secularized religiosity. He identifies structural parallels with
  classical mystical traditions (union with the Absolute, loss of self in
  the divine) while distinguishing Fichte's idealist inflection.

  RELEVANCE: This is the central English-language study of the religious
  character of Fichte's mature text on blessedness. It directly addresses
  the question of whether Fichte's divine Absolute constitutes a genuinely
  religious or merely philosophical concept, and whether "blessedness" is
  experiential in a sense that goes beyond ethical fulfillment.

  POSITION: Religious/mystical turn reading -- the later Fichte's doctrine
  of the Absolute represents a substantial departure from the Jena ethics
  toward something closer to speculative mysticism.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, religion, Absolute, blessed-life, mysticism, High},
  abstract = {Fichte’s The Way towards the Blessed Life is a genuinely mystical work that contains several themes characteristic of mystical writing: the opposition of a non-spatio-temporal, non-manifold being to the world as it appears; the ineffability of the Divine; the centrality of union with God and of detachment; and the individual as a conduit for Divine life and love. It must, however, be granted that Fichte conjoins his affirmations of union with denials that the ontological identity of human beings and God is a matter of experience. This is nevertheless an insufficient reason for denying that Fichte’s text is correctly characterized as mystical, for experience of God’s presence is a more adequate criterion of the mystical than the experience of ontological identity or even of union with God, and the experiences that The Way towards the Blessed Life does acknowledge are properly interpreted in terms of Divine presence.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{estes2016atheism,
  author = {Bowman, Curtis and Estes, Yolanda},
  editor = {Estes, Yolanda},
  title = {{J.G.} {F}ichte and the Atheism Dispute (1798--1800)},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  doi = {10.4324/9781315590226},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This anthology collects and contextualizes the primary
  texts of the Atheismusstreit together with scholarly commentary. The
  editorial apparatus traces how Fichte's essay "On the Ground of our
  Belief in a Divine World-Governance" (1798) -- which identified God with
  the moral world-order rather than with a personal deity -- triggered the
  accusation of atheism and Fichte's dismissal from Jena. The volume shows
  the philosophical stakes: whether a moral-teleological concept of God is
  compatible with Christianity and whether transcendental idealism entails
  atheism.

  RELEVANCE: This is the authoritative English-language scholarly resource
  on the Atheismusstreit. For the philosophy of history, the controversy
  matters because it forced Fichte to reconceive the divine Absolute in
  ways that directly shaped the post-Jena period -- including the
  theological framework of the Characteristics and the Blessed Life.

  POSITION: Atheismusstreit as philosophical turning-point -- the
  controversy as the crucible in which Fichte's mature theology of history
  was forged.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Atheismusstreit, atheism, divine-world-governance, religion, High},
  abstract = {The atheism dispute is one of the most important philosophical controversies of late eighteenth and early nineteenth century Germany. Johann Gottlieb Fichte, one of the leading philosophers of the period, was accused of atheism after publishing his essay 'On the Ground of Our Belief in a Divine World-Governance', which he had written in response to Karl Friedrich Forberg's essay 'Development of the Concept of Religion'. Fichte argued that recognition of the moral law includes affirmation of a 'moral world order', which he identified with God. Critics charged both Forberg and Fichte with atheism, thereby prompting Fichte to launch a public campaign of defense that included his threat to resign his position at the University of Jena if he were subjected to any government reprimand. Fichte was forced to make good this threat when his work was censured. The dispute eventually died down but it influenced many other thinkers for years to come. J. G. Fichte: The Atheism Dispute (1798-1800) is the first English commentary devoted solely to the atheism dispute as well as the first English translation of collected writings from the Atheism Dispute. This book brings together many major essays and documents relating to this dispute. These include the anonymous polemic 'A Father's Letter to his Student Son about Fichte's and Forberg's Atheism', Fichte's essays 'Appeal to the Public' and 'Juridical Defense', and numerous documents from the University of Jena and the ducal courts of Dresden, Weimar, and Gotha. Most of the texts are translated from German into English for the first time, and all are accompanied by full commentaries and detailed notes. Bowman and Estes bring to an English speaking audience the full details of this controversy, which ended Fichte's career in Jena and profoundly influenced his approach to communicating philosophical and religious concepts.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{rosales2017gottliche,
  author = {Rivera de Rosales, Jacinto},
  title = {{Das G\"{o}ttliche. Der Atheismusstreit und die Wende im Denken Fichtes}},
  journal = {Fichte-Studien},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {44},
  pages = {31--48},
  doi = {10.5840/fichte2017445},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rosales traces the philosophical development from the
  Atheismusstreit to Fichte's post-1800 thought, arguing that the
  controversy was not merely a political embarrassment but a genuine
  philosophical turning-point. The article shows how the accusation of
  atheism -- and Jacobi's charge of nihilism -- pushed Fichte from
  conceiving the divine as moral world-order to developing a richer
  metaphysics of absolute Being or God as the ontological foundation
  of the Wissenschaftslehre.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question of whether Fichte's
  mature philosophy of history rests on a "secularized providence" or
  a genuine theological reconstitution. Rosales argues the latter:
  the shift from moral order to Absolute Being transforms the
  providential framework structuring Fichte's historical thought.

  POSITION: Atheismusstreit as turning-point, with emphasis on
  genuine theological reconstitution of the Absolute.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Atheismusstreit, Absolute, divine, turning-point, High},
  abstract = {The question of God or the Divine has essential influenced the development of Fichtian thinking and guided it from transcendental I as the first principle in the Jena period to absolute being or God and his absolute phenomenon or manifestation. The turning point came with the so-called atheism dispute due to his article »On the Ground of our Belief in a divine World-Governance« (Fall 1798), which caused him so much trouble. In this article, Fichte defended the idea that the divine is not a substance or object, not a person, so no theistic God, but the moral order – a notion that could not be accepted by the Christian Church. The criticism that he is either atheist or pantheist or nihilist or a merely subjective thinker has greatly hit him and changed his horizon of thought in line with the new more religious spirit of his cultural environment.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{crowe2008revisionism,
  author = {Crowe, Benjamin D.},
  title = {Revisionism and Religion in {F}ichte's {J}ena {Wissenschaftslehre}},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2008},
  volume = {16},
  number = {2},
  pages = {371--392},
  doi = {10.1080/09608780801969183},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Crowe examines how Fichte's Jena-period philosophy of
  religion was shaped by his revisionary reading of Kant's Critical
  philosophy. Rather than abandoning religion, the Jena Fichte sought
  to place it on a new foundation: the moral world-order grounded in
  the practical I. Crowe argues that this "revisionist" theology --
  defending religion without speculative theism -- directly set up
  the tensions that erupted in the Atheismusstreit.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the philosophical background for Fichte's
  theological development by showing how the seeds of the
  Atheismusstreit lay in the systematic commitments of the Jena period.
  Understanding this context is necessary for situating the post-1800
  reconstitution of the divine-historical nexus.

  POSITION: Continuity reading with attention to the internal tensions
  that the Jena system contained regarding religion.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, religion, Wissenschaftslehre, Jena-period, Kant, Medium},
  abstract = {The relationship between philosophical reflection and religious belief has been an issue since the very earliest times. One popular view of this relation is revisionism. Philosophical revisionists ...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{martin2008transcendental,
  author = {Martin, Wayne M.},
  title = {Transcendental Philosophy and Atheism},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {16},
  number = {1},
  pages = {109--130},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1468-0378.2007.00271.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Martin analyzes the logical relationship between
  transcendental idealism and atheism, focusing on the charges brought
  against Fichte. He argues that the accusation misunderstood Fichte's
  position: Fichte's identification of God with the moral world-order
  is not atheism but rather a form of ethical theology consistent with
  (if not identical to) Kant's practical postulates. Martin reconstructs
  the philosophical debate in terms of the conditions of reference for
  religious claims.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most rigorous analytical treatment of whether
  Fichte's conception of divine world-governance in 1798-99 was
  genuinely atheistic or merely non-theistic. This is directly relevant
  to assessing whether the "world-plan" constitutes a secularized
  providence or a genuine theological claim.

  POSITION: Secularized theodicy reading -- Fichte's divine moral order
  as compatible with (transformed) theism rather than atheistic.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, atheism, transcendental-idealism, divine-governance, moral-order, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{ahlers2003vitalism,
  author = {Ahlers, Rolf},
  title = {Vitalism and System: {J}acobi and {F}ichte on Philosophy and Life},
  journal = {Idealistic Studies},
  year = {2003},
  volume = {33},
  number = {1},
  pages = {83--113},
  doi = {10.5840/idstudies20033313},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ahlers compares Jacobi and Fichte on the relationship
  between philosophical system and life, with particular attention to
  the theological stakes of the Atheismusstreit. He argues that Jacobi's
  charge of nihilism (as opposed to mere atheism) against Fichte's system
  reflects a deeper disagreement about whether a systematic philosophy of
  the Absolute can capture the living relation to God. Fichte's response
  involves an appeal to the life of the I as the locus of the divine.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates how the Jacobi-Fichte debate about nihilism and
  the living Absolute shapes Fichte's later philosophy of the Absolute as
  life. This is background for understanding why Fichte's mature historical
  teleology is framed in terms of life and love rather than mere systematic
  necessity.

  POSITION: Atheismusstreit context, with attention to the Jacobi
  challenge that pushed Fichte toward a vitalist conception of the Absolute.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Jacobi, nihilism, vitalism, Absolute, Atheismusstreit, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper thematizes the crucial agreement and point of departure between Jacobi and Fichte at the height of the “atheism controversy.” The argument on the proper relationship between philosophy and existence or speculation and life had far-reaching consequences in the history of thought after Jacobi and Fichte in German Idealism on the one hand, primarly advocated by Schelling and Hegel, and on the other hand by existentialism and vitalism. The essay focuses first on Jacobi’s philosophy of life, which centrally influenced and attracted Fichte to Jacobi. Jacobi’s dualism between speculation, of which he was skeptical, and life, became Fichte’s dualism. Fichte’s transcendentalism, however, prioritized, contrary to Jacobi, both speculation and systematicity. Both of these elements became central for later forms of German Idealism. In the last part of the essay Hegel’s absolute idealism becomes the platform affording a critical perspective on Fichte’s transcendental philosophy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{gaspar2016nihilismus,
  author = {Gaspar, Francisco Prata},
  title = {{Entweder Gott oder Nichts -- Nihilismus und transzendentaler Idealismus}},
  booktitle = {{Fichte und seine Zeit}},
  year = {2016},
  pages = {158--171},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004319820_011},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gaspar examines Jacobi's "Letter to Fichte" during the
  Atheismusstreit, in which Jacobi characterized Fichte's Wissenschaftslehre
  as nihilism rather than atheism. The article analyzes Fichte's defense --
  the distinction between the point of view of life and the point of view
  of philosophy -- and argues that Fichte's response, while internally
  consistent, ultimately vindicates the idealist claim that transcendental
  philosophy cannot yield knowledge of God as an object.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the nihilism charge, which is philosophically
  more serious than the atheism charge for Fichte's philosophy of history:
  if the Absolute is merely a construct of the Wissenschaftslehre, the
  divine world-plan lacks ontological grounding.

  POSITION: Atheismusstreit context, examining the nihilism charge's
  implications for Fichte's conception of the Absolute.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Jacobi, nihilism, Absolute, Atheismusstreit, Medium},
  abstract = {Either God or nothing – Nihilism and transcendental Idealism. By the time of the “Atheism- Controversy” (Atheismusstreit) Jacobi wrote a “Letter to Fichte” (Brief an Fichte) in which he described the doctrine of science as Nihilism and not as Atheism. According to Jacobi, the doctrine of science would incur in to an emptiness of meaning and in to an artificial belief. Nevertheless, Fichte’s first and immediate position towards those critiques was not one of perplexity, because according to him the critiques Jacobi’s on the doctrine of science were in fact to be understood as a confusion between life and philosophy. The aim here is to discuss the distinction Fichte’s between the point of view of life and the one of the philosophy. This contribution isn’t however based only on texts that approach this distinction, but also analyses it in an illustrative way by interpreting some speculative writings of Fichte himself, specially the published exposition of his doctrine of science: the Grundlage der gesamten Wissenschaftslehre. Those texts apparently reinforce Jacobi’s position, but show in fact that Fichte is right. This way it may possible to reach a clear description of transcendental idealism, or at least as it was presented by Fichte in his Jena-period (1794–99).},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{hoeltzel2014theistic,
  author = {Hoeltzel, Steven},
  title = {Transcendental Idealism and Theistic Commitment in {F}ichte},
  booktitle = {The Palgrave Handbook of German Idealism},
  year = {2014},
  pages = {364--385},
  publisher = {Palgrave Macmillan UK},
  doi = {10.1007/978-1-137-33475-6_19},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hoeltzel examines whether Fichte's transcendental
  idealism entails, permits, or excludes theistic commitment. He argues
  that Fichte's system -- especially in the post-Jena period -- supports
  a form of theism: the Absolute is not merely a regulative idea but the
  ontological ground of the Wissenschaftslehre, and finite consciousness
  is a "manifestation" or "appearance" of this Absolute. The chapter
  surveys both early and late texts.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a comprehensive overview of the theistic dimension
  of Fichte's idealism, situating the question of divine providence in the
  context of his broader metaphysics. Useful for understanding how the
  "world-plan" relates to the Absolute in Fichte's mature system.

  POSITION: Religious/mystical turn reading -- the later Fichte as
  genuinely committed to theistic metaphysics.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, theism, Absolute, idealism, religion, Medium},
  abstract = {What does Fichte's philosophy, especially in writings relating to the "atheism dispute [Atheismusstreit]" (1798–1800), actually say about the intellectual basis and philosophical tenability of theistic belief? Fichte found himself at odds with his own audience on exactly this issue,1 and puzzlement on this point persists to this day. In 1798, he published the chapter "On the Basis of Our Belief in a Divine Governance of the World." This text's derivation of pre-philosophical religious conviction from ineluctable acts of ideation, coupled with its identification of God with the moral world order, can give the impression (and prompted the objection) that Fichte philosophically accounts for belief in God in a way that proves to be completely corrosive of any genuinely theistic commitment. And his attempts to rebut that objection, especially in Book III of 1800's The Vocation of Man (hereafter, "VM III"), depict and defend properly philosophical belief in a supreme being in a way that has perplexed and divided scholars right down to the present, and so seriously as to raise doubts about how well we have understood the essential content of his Jena-era transcendental idealism — from which, according to Fichte himself, The Vocation of Man does not radically depart.2},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{scarfia2025trial,
  author = {Scarfia, Lucas Dami{\'a}n},
  title = {Fichte on Trial. The Atheist, the Nihilist, the Democrat},
  journal = {Revista de Estud(i)os sobre Fichte},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {29},
  doi = {10.4000/15e9p},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Scarfia reconstructs the three accusations leveled at
  Fichte in the Atheismusstreit -- atheism, nihilism, and democratic
  Jacobinism -- arguing that each has some basis in Fichte's texts. The
  article shows how Fichte's practical idealism, which grounds God in
  the moral order rather than independent existence, and his political
  radicalism were perceived as mutually reinforcing threats by his
  contemporaries.

  RELEVANCE: Contextualizes the Atheismusstreit as the intersection of
  political and theological criticism, relevant to assessing how far the
  post-Jena Fichte's divine world-plan was a response to political as
  well as philosophical pressure.

  POSITION: Atheismusstreit as turning-point, with attention to the
  political-theological nexus.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Atheismusstreit, nihilism, atheism, Jacobinism, Medium},
  abstract = {AbstractIn this article, I aim to elucidate the meaning of the accusations that J. G. Fichte faced in the context of the Atheism Dispute (Atheismusstreit), according to which his idealistic philosophy was deemed atheistic. In this framework, he was further accused of nihilism and, by his own reading of the charges, branded as a Jacobin-democrat. The goal is to identify in his works—Grundlage der gesamten Wissenschaftslehre (1794-1795) as well as the Revolutionsschriften (1793-1794) and the texts written during the Dispute (1798-1799)—the conceptual nodes on which these labels are based. In this regard, reference is also made to his philosophical relationship with F. H. Jacobi, who played a significant role in the Dispute: Jacobi both criticized Fichte and delineated a dichotomy between his own religious thinking and Fichte’s moral-idealism. The purpose of this analysis is twofold: to shed light on Fichte’s philosophical ideas, and to argue that the labels attributed to him and his philosophy have, in fact, a certain basis even if Fichte sometimes sought to dissociate himself from them. This examination highlights the metaphysical distinction between idealistic and dogmatic philosophy, as well as the differences in their practical implications, ultimately vindicating idealism as a philosophy of freedom and equality.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{napoli2020novalis,
  author = {Napoli, Santiago},
  title = {{El profesor, el te{\'o}logo y el pol{\'i}tico. Novalis frente al Atheismusstreit de Fichte}},
  journal = {Revista de Estud(i)os sobre Fichte},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {21},
  doi = {10.4000/ref.1614},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Napoli examines Novalis's philosophical, theological, and
  political readings of the Atheismusstreit, arguing that Novalis had
  a detailed and nuanced understanding of the controversy and expressed
  considerable sympathy with Fichte's position. The article draws on
  Novalis's correspondence and key works of 1799, situating the
  controversy within the broader Romantic theological-political context.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the reception history of the Atheismusstreit within
  Early German Romanticism, showing how the theological debate about
  Fichte's divine world-governance resonated with contemporaries who were
  simultaneously developing their own versions of a philosophy of history
  grounded in the divine. This contextualizes Fichte's position within
  wider post-Kantian debates.

  POSITION: Atheismusstreit context -- reception and contemporary debate.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Novalis, Atheismusstreit, Romanticism, context, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper aims to inquire Novalis’ philosophical, theological and political readings about the controversy that involved Johann Gottlieb Fichte and ended up with his removal as a profesor at the University of Jena, the so-called Atheismusstreit. The article’s main thesis statement affirms that Novalis possesses a truly detailed knowledge of the discussions within this controversy. At the same time, and with certain nuances, he exhibites a considerable political and philosophical affinity with Fichte’s plea. In order to demonstrate this statement, we examine Novalis’ correspondence, alongside two of his main works: Das Allgemeine Brouillon. Materialien zur Enzyklopadistik and Die Christenheit oder Europa, both belonging to 1799, the year in which the controversy primarily took place.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{klotz2014being,
  author = {Klotz, Christian},
  title = {Being and the Life of Consciousness in {F}ichte's Late Philosophy (1810/1811)},
  journal = {Rivista di Storia della Filosofia},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {69},
  number = {4},
  pages = {639--647},
  doi = {10.3280/SF2014-004005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Klotz analyzes the Fichte of 1810-1811, focusing on
  the relationship between Being (das Sein) and the life of consciousness
  in the final versions of the Wissenschaftslehre. He argues that in the
  late Fichte, the Absolute reveals itself through the "appearance"
  (Erscheinung) of consciousness, and that this structure of
  self-manifestation provides the ontological key to the late religious
  philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Directly relevant to the question of how finite reason
  mediates its relation to the divine in Fichte's mature system. The
  doctrine of appearance as the manifestation of absolute Being is the
  metaphysical framework within which Fichte's account of history as
  the progressive self-revelation of the divine must be understood.

  POSITION: Religious/mystical turn reading -- the Absolute as
  genuinely ontological ground of the late system.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Absolute, Being, consciousness, late-philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {In continuity with his early position, also in the late formulations of the Science of Knowledge Fichte maintains the idea that the deduction and elucidation of the structure of consciousness is an essential task of philosophy. This idea is clearly present in the introductory lectures of 1810/11, Facts of Consciousness, and in the version of the Science of Knowledge which Fichte presented in 1811. The article aims to identify the central tenets involved in Fichte’s two investigations of consciousness presented in 1810/11. The Author claims that both in Facts of Consciousness and in the Science of Knowledge of 1811, Fichte develops a monist conception which describes the fundamental activity of consciousness in non-egological terms. However, there are two different kinds of monism involved in Fichte’s two accounts. In Facts of Consciousness, Fichte defends a structural monism which focusses on the structurally identical, objectifying activity which is present in all individual instances of consciousness. In contrast, the 1811 Science of Knowledge is based on the conception of pure as the ultimate principle of philosophy, which implies that consciousness must be understood as mere image of being. The Author argues that whereas Fichte’s introductory account investigates the self-structuring activity of consciousness which gives rise to an objective worldview, the monism of being adopted in Fichte’s ultimate foundation of philosophy concerns the ontological status of consciousness as being that of an image-like, secondary mode of being. Thus, in 1810/11 Fichte understands consciousness as being self-structuring without holding it to be ontologically absolute.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{oren2025maimon,
  author = {Oren, Yady},
  title = {Maimon and {F}ichte on the Impossibility of Philosophical Atheism},
  journal = {Teologia i Moralno{\'s}{\'c}},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {20},
  pages = {167--175},
  doi = {10.14746/tim.2025.39.2.13},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Oren investigates the shared position of Salomon Maimon
  and Fichte on the impossibility of philosophical atheism, despite both
  having faced accusations of atheism. The article analyzes Fichte's 1799
  essay "On the Basis of Our Belief in a Divine Governance of the World"
  to show that for Fichte, atheism is not merely false but conceptually
  self-contradictory: God as moral necessity grounded in practical reason
  cannot be coherently denied without abandoning practical rationality
  itself.

  RELEVANCE: This recent paper provides the clearest statement of how
  Fichte's 1799 essay understands divine governance as a practical
  necessity rather than a theoretical postulate. This is directly relevant
  to the question of whether the "world-plan" of the Characteristics is
  a secularized providence or retains genuine theological content.

  POSITION: Secularized theodicy reading -- divine governance as
  rationally necessary rather than revealed or metaphysically independent.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, Maimon, atheism, divine-governance, practical-reason, Low},
  abstract = {This article investigates the shared position of Salomon Maimon and Johann Gottlieb Fichte on the impossibility of philosophical atheism, despite the historical accusations of atheism directed at both thinkers. The method employed is a close comparative reading of Maimon’s Philosophisches Wörterbuch (1791) and Fichte’s On the Basis of Our Belief in a Divine Governance of the World (1799), contextualized within their biographical and cultural backgrounds. The analysis shows that while both thinkers reject traditional proofs for God’s existence, they affirm the concept of God as either a logically necessary idea (Maimon) or a moral necessity grounded in practical reason (Fichte). The article concludes that for both philosophers, atheism is not merely false but conceptually self-contradictory, and that this shared stance reflects a post-Kantian transformation of theology from theoretical metaphysics to practical reason.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{nini2024fichteberlin,
  author    = {Nini, Matthew},
  title     = {Fichte in Berlin},
  year      = {2024},
  publisher = {McGill-Queen's University Press},
  doi       = {10.1515/9780228021520},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nini offers a sustained reading of Fichte's philosophical output during the Berlin period (1804--1806), focusing on the second set of 1804 lectures on the Wissenschaftslehre. He argues that the WL-1804-II deploys a fivefold transcendental argument structure -- not a simple turn to the Absolute at the expense of transcendental philosophy -- and that this structure exhibits both continuity with the Jena method and a genuine doctrinal innovation in the account of how knowing relates to being.

  RELEVANCE: Directly bears on whether the Berlin period Wissenschaftslehre represents a break or development relative to Jena, which is one of the domain's key questions. Nini's argument for structural continuity in method (see also his 2023 Fichte-Studien article) while acknowledging doctrinal transformation is one of the most careful recent treatments of the Jena/Berlin periodization.

  POSITION: Occupies the methodological-continuity-with-doctrinal-transformation camp in the continuity/rupture debate.
  },
  keywords  = {fichte-1804, berlin-period, wissenschaftslehre-absolute, jena-berlin-continuity, High},
  abstract = {When the celebrated German philosopher Johann Gottlieb Fichte lost his position at the University of Jena and moved to Berlin, it looked as if his career was over. In 1799 Berlin had no university, and Fichte was consigned to lecturing in his home. In Fichte in Berlin Matthew Nini breaks with scholarly consensus, arguing it was there that Fichte finally reached maturity, and the only way to understand Fichte’s mature philosophy is to perform it for oneself. The book focuses on the philosopher’s 1804 lectures on the Wissenschaftslehre – an untranslatable neologism for his theories on the pursuit of insight – claiming that they are one of the most exemplary versions of the philosophical project that Fichte reconfigured some seventeen times throughout his life. While the 1804 lectures offer a more robust approach, they remain faithful to the insight at the heart of the original philosophy. Fichte’s work always emphasized the practical over the theoretical, and his 1804 work goes even further: to think with Fichte is to bring one’s own philosophy to life. Nini guides the reader step by step through the complex arguments Fichte made in 1804 and goes on to examine some of his other works produced in their wake, arguing that Fichte’s output from 1804 to 1806, his first Berlin period, forms an organic whole. Fichte in Berlin is not only an introduction to Fichte’s later philosophy, but also an original philosophical work that makes a unique contribution to the study of German Idealism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{bruno2021facticitygenesissis,
  author    = {Bruno, G.~Anthony},
  title     = {Facticity and Genesis: Tracking Fichte's Method in the Berlin Wissenschaftslehre},
  booktitle = {The Enigma of Fichte's First Principles},
  year      = {2021},
  pages     = {177--197},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi       = {10.1163/9789004459793_010},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bruno traces how Fichte coins 'facticity' in his Berlin period to address a problem already present in Jena: the contingency of the forms of experience that Kant's metaphysical deduction leaves ungrounded. He argues for continuity in the genetic method across the Jena and Berlin periods -- both aim to derive necessarily what Kant left as given -- while noting a methodological shift in the Berlin period whereby Fichte applies the critique to his own doctrine of intellectual intuition in order to eliminate residual factical terms from it.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most detailed textual case for the continuity-of-method thesis across the Jena/Berlin divide, directly addressing the domain's question about whether the later Wissenschaftslehre fundamentally transforms or refines the earlier system. Bruno's distinction between methodological and doctrinal continuity is a key analytical tool for the project.

  POSITION: Defends methodological continuity while acknowledging targeted doctrinal revision, challenging both strong continuity and rupture theses.
  },
  keywords  = {jena-berlin-continuity, facticity-genesis, wissenschaftslehre-method, High},
  abstract = {The concept of facticity denotes conditions of experience whose necessity is not logical yet whose contingency is not empirical. Although often associated with Heidegger, Fichte coins ‘facticity’ in his Berlin period to refer to the conclusion of Kant’s metaphysical deduction of the categories, which he argues leaves it a contingent matter that we have the conditions of experience that we do. Such rhapsodic or factical conditions, he argues, must follow necessarily, independent of empirical givenness, from the I through a process of ‘genesis.’ I reconstruct Fichte’s argument by (1) tracing the origin of his neologism, (2) presenting his Jena critique of Kant’s rhapsodic appeal to the forms of judgment, and (3) illustrating the Jena period’s continuity with the Berlin period’s genetic method, while noting a methodological shift whereby Fichte directs his critique against his own doctrine of intellectual intuition in order to eliminate its ‘factical terms.’},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{kosch2025jenaredenphilosophical,
  author   = {Kosch, Michelle},
  title    = {From the Jena period to the {Reden}: Philosophical and historical developments},
  journal  = {Deutsche Zeitschrift f{\"u}r Philosophie},
  year     = {2025},
  volume   = {73},
  number   = {1},
  pages    = {70--81},
  doi      = {10.1515/dzph-2025-0005},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kosch examines the philosophical and historical developments connecting the Jena period Wissenschaftslehre to the Reden an die deutsche Nation, arguing that the transition involves both genuine philosophical developments and changes in Fichte's historical-political context. She tracks how specific doctrines from the Jena period (including the practical primacy of the I and the structure of rational community) are transformed in the Berlin writings, attending to what is continuous and what genuinely shifts.

  RELEVANCE: A recent (2025) contribution that bears directly on the periodization question and the systematic connections between the Wissenschaftslehre and Fichte's philosophy of history, serving as a counterpoint to both strong continuity and discontinuity positions.

  POSITION: Nuanced periodization work tracking both continuity and philosophical development across the Jena/Berlin divide.
  },
  keywords = {jena-berlin-continuity, reden, fichte-history, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract This paper surveys the major themes of Fichte’s Reden an die deutsche Nation with a view to situating them within Fichte’s earlier practical philosophy. In these lectures Fichte defines ‘nation’; defends a principle for determining a nation’s true (“inner”) borders; argues for a view of the relation of culture to language; argues for the superiority of the German to the French language as a vehicle for culture; offers an account of why an individual should be concerned with the survival of his nation and with the progress of its culture; argues that political independence is a necessary condition of the survival of a nation; argues that education is the only path to political independence (for Germans in their historical situation); and outlines a system of education aimed in part at advancing that end. Little of this is prefigured in the practical philosophy of the Jena period, though much is consistent with it, and some is prefigured by certain of Fichte’s writings post-1800.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{rametta2026wissenschaftslehrestaatslehre,
  author   = {Rametta, Gaetano},
  title    = {{``Wissenschaftslehre''} and {``Staatslehre''} in {Fichte}'s Late Works},
  journal  = {Fichte Studien},
  year     = {2026},
  volume   = {54},
  number   = {2},
  pages    = {285--302},
  doi      = {10.1163/18795811-05401016},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rametta analyzes the structural unity between the Wissenschaftslehre and the Staatslehre (1813) in Fichte's late works, arguing that the multiplicity of presentations of the Wissenschaftslehre reflects the dynamic self-manifestation of the Absolute through consciousness. The Staatslehre introduces Christ as the absolute historical fact that enables a shift from pre-modern political theology to a post-Christian order grounded in rational self-determination; sovereignty is reinterpreted as the unfolding of transcendental intelligence within a community.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the relation between the late Wissenschaftslehre and Fichte's philosophy of history and politics, showing how the turn to the Absolute in the later system is not merely speculative but entails a concrete theory of historical transformation and political legitimacy.

  POSITION: Argues for deep systematic unity between late speculative and political works, grounding Fichte's philosophy of history in the ontology of the Absolute's self-manifestation.
  },
  keywords = {fichte-history, staatslehre, wissenschaftslehre-absolute, late-fichte, High},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 
 The paper analyzes the structural unity between
 Wissenschaftslehre
 and
 Staatslehre
 in Fichte’s late works, emphasizing their convergence in a philosophical project that integrates speculative insight and political transformation. The multiplicity of expositions of the
 Wissenschaftslehre
 is interpreted as an essential feature of its identity, grounded in the dynamic self-manifestation of the Absolute through consciousness. Philosophical insight (
 Einsicht
 ) becomes the medium through which theory is transfigured into life and action. The
 Staatslehre
 introduces Christ as the absolute historical fact that enables a shift from a pre-modern political theology based on miracles and coercion to a post-Christian order grounded in freedom and rational self-determination. Sovereignty, traditionally legitimized by revelation or contract, is reconfigured as the unfolding of transcendental intelligence within a community. The dissolution of theological–political authority culminates in a constitutional model in which reason itself governs. In this framework, the
 Wissenschaftslehre
 emerges as both speculative doctrine and political constitution, replacing faith with self-conscious insight as the foundation of political legitimacy.
},
}

@article{neumann2023waseinheitabsoluten,
  author   = {Neumann, Fridolin},
  title    = {Was ist die Einheit des Absoluten?},
  journal  = {Fichte Studien},
  year     = {2023},
  volume   = {52},
  number   = {2},
  pages    = {503--522},
  doi      = {10.1163/18795811-05202010},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Neumann argues that the 1804-II Wissenschaftslehre's claim to continue and correct Kant's transcendental philosophy is implausible because Fichte's altered premises -- in particular his demand for an absolute unity undifferentiated in itself -- diverge fundamentally from Kant's. He shows that Fichte's objection that Kant established three absolutes rather than one is based on this Fichtean demand for absolute unity, and that this demand reflects a departure from transcendental philosophy into a different metaphysical register.

  RELEVANCE: Sheds light on the rupture within the Wissenschaftslehre itself between the Jena (transcendental) and Berlin (quasi-Spinozist/absolute) orientations, directly relevant to the question of how the turn to the Absolute transforms the basis of Fichte's historical thought.

  POSITION: Represents the discontinuity thesis at the level of the Wissenschaftslehre itself, arguing for a genuine metaphysical transformation in the 1804 lectures.
  },
  keywords = {fichte-1804, wissenschaftslehre-absolute, kant-fichte, discontinuity, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
In the Wissenschaftslehre 1804-II, Fichte claims to both continue and correct Kant’s transcendental philosophy. I reject this double claim by demonstrating that Fichte’s engagement with Kant is guided by altered premises that make the affiliation to Kant’s transcendental philosophy seem implausible. These divergences culminate in an opposing understanding of unity between the two philosophers, which can be revealed by attention to Fichte’s objections to the KdU. Fichte’s thesis that Kant established three absolutes instead of one absolute is based on Fichte’s own demand for an absolute unity that is not differentiated in itself, which is also reflected in his transposition of Kant’s concept of the root to the KdU. In section 2, I attempt a precise exegetical reconstruction of Fichte’s concept of the absolute, as well as of his thesis of Kant’s triple absolute. In section 3, I raise three interconnected objections to this: first, Fichte’s critique is actually based on an accusation of dualism, which is suggested both by his own concept of the absolute and the programme of the KdU (section 3.1). Second, however, Kant’s two absoluta should be seen more as constructions by Fichte rather than as revealing problems immanent to Kant’s philosophy (section 3.2). Third, these divergences can be traced back to Fichte’s own understanding of unity, which gives rise to his objections to the KdU in the first place (section 3.3).},
}

@article{zoller2023logologiafichtekantian,
  author   = {Z{\"o}ller, G{\"u}nter},
  title    = {``{Logologia.}'' {Fichte's} Kantian Self-Interpretation of the {Wissenschaftslehre} in the ``Aphorisms on the Essence of Philosophy as Science'' from 1804},
  journal  = {Fichte Studien},
  year     = {2023},
  volume   = {52},
  number   = {2},
  pages    = {544--561},
  doi      = {10.1163/18795811-05202012},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zoller introduces and analyzes an overlooked 1804 text by Fichte in which he interprets his own philosophical project as continuous with Kant's transcendental philosophy, arguing both use a 'transcendentalist' method opposed to psychologism. Zoller reads both Kant's and Fichte's core initiatives as centered on a novel way of doing philosophy rather than a set of metaphysical doctrines, and traces this self-interpretation to a logicist or normative-epistemological reading of transcendental philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates how Fichte himself understood the continuity of the Wissenschaftslehre across its many presentations in 1804, and complicates rupture narratives by showing Fichte's own self-understanding at the very moment of supposed transformation.

  POSITION: Supports a reading of Fichte's later self-interpretation as continuous with transcendental idealism rather than departing into a different metaphysics.
  },
  keywords = {fichte-1804, wissenschaftslehre-method, kant-fichte, self-interpretation, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
The contribution introduces a virtually unknown, but significant text by Fichte dating from 1804, entitled “Aphorisms on the Essence of Philosophy as Science”. This text summarizes the main tenets of his philosophical life project, the Wissenschaftslehre. In particular, the contribution relates Fichte’s auto-interpretive remarks to the self-interpretation of his principal predecessor, Kant. Both Kant’s and Fichte’s core philosophical initiatives are shown to center around a novel way of doing philosophy, for which Kant uses the term “transcendental philosophy” and Fichte employs the related designation “transcendentalism”. The contribution is organized in two sections. The first section is devoted to Kant’s programmatic introduction of transcendental philosophy in the Critique of Pure Reason (1781, 1787). The second section is dedicated to Fichte’s revisions of Kant’s transcendental philosophy, as undertaken in successive presentations of the Wissenschaftslehre and succinctly summarized in the “Aphorisms” from 1804. Particular attention is paid to Fichte’s critical assessment of Kant’s execution of their common transcendental project. The contribution argues for a de-psychologized and logicist interpretation of the transcendental project in Kant and Fichte, which finds a suitable successor in the neo-Kantian (and neo-Fichtean) development of axiological or normative epistemology (Erkenntnistheorie) as foundational philosophical discipline in nineteenth and twentieth century philosophy.},
}

@article{strasser2023wissenschaftslehreentfaltung,
  author   = {Strasser, Franz},
  title    = {Die {Wissenschaftslehre} als (Selbst-)Entfaltung der Rationalit{\"a}t am Beispiel des 27. Vortrages der WL-1804-{II}},
  journal  = {Fichte Studien},
  year     = {2023},
  volume   = {52},
  number   = {2},
  pages    = {523--543},
  doi      = {10.1163/18795811-05202011},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Strasser reads the 1804-II Wissenschaftslehre as a self-development of rationality through the structure of evidence (Evidenz) and genetic inference, arguing that reason and freedom are mutually dependent in the true image of being. The 27th lecture serves as the focus for reconstructing how Fichte conceives the Wissenschaftslehre as the self-unfolding of rational self-knowledge, not merely a description of the Absolute from without.

  RELEVANCE: Helps clarify the internal logic of the 1804 Wissenschaftslehre's claim to be a systematic self-development -- a structure that, if successful, could provide the basis for understanding historical development as the self-unfolding of rationality in time.

  POSITION: Sympathetic reading of the 1804 WL as achieving a coherent account of rational self-manifestation, supporting the systematic unity thesis.
  },
  keywords = {fichte-1804, rationality-self-development, wissenschaftslehre-absolute, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
The second edition of the 1804 Wissenschaftslehre purports to be a self-development of rationality, a rational knowledge of knowledge. The ‘rational’ refers a) to the mind and b) to a specific form of objectification called evidence of ‘reason’. In the first part of the lecture series, Fichte refers to the entire course as ‘a doctrine of reason and truth’ (15th lecture, ibid., p. 150), and second, as a ‘doctrine of appearance and illusion’, but one that is indeed true and is grounded in truth (15th lecture, ibid., pp. 150–151). The theory of appearances is a second ascent: this is because in the facticity of an appearance, the genesis of a certain reason-consequent relationship can be asserted. The synthesis of reason and freedom are thus mutually dependent in the true image of being (‘Bild-Wirklichkeit’) and in the knowledge of a genetic relationship. The focus of the investigation is the 27th lecture, but other lectures are consulted for understanding.},
}

@article{nini2023fichtefivefoldtranscendental,
  author   = {Nini, Matthew},
  title    = {Fichte's Fivefold Transcendental Argument in the {WL}-1804-{II}},
  journal  = {Fichte Studien},
  year     = {2023},
  volume   = {52},
  number   = {1},
  pages    = {156--174},
  doi      = {10.1163/18795811-05201009},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nini argues that the 1804-II Wissenschaftslehre offers a theory of transcendental arguments and is itself a protracted transcendental argument. The basic form -- agreement between what is said and what is enacted -- is expanded from a twofold to a fivefold structure to prove that the transcendental argument is genetic (not presupposing an implicit third term) and that the standpoint of argumentation must be internal to the argument itself.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a technical account of the argumentative structure of the 1804 WL that is directly relevant to assessing whether the turn to the Absolute represents a methodological rupture or a refinement of the transcendental project. Complements Nini's book-length treatment.

  POSITION: Argues for structural continuity of the transcendental method in the 1804 WL, complicating rupture narratives.
  },
  keywords = {fichte-1804, transcendental-argument, wissenschaftslehre-method, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
This article argues that Fichte’s second set of lectures on the Wissenschaftslehre in 1804 offers a theory of transcendental arguments, and is itself a protracted transcendental argument. Foundationally, it employs the transcendental argument form that is common to much of Fichte’s work: the agreement of a truth-statement (what is said, sagen) and its enactment (what it does, tun). WL-1804-II will expand this twofold structure into a threefold and then into a fivefold one. The first expansion arises from the need to prove that the transcendental argument of the WL is genetic: it does not introduce an implicit third term between the content of a proposition and its enactment in discourse. The argument must be able to move from the immediacy of experience (what Fichte will call Evidenz), to its elaboration in discourse, and then back to the initial experience. What is elaborated in discourse is what is found in experience. The expansion into a fivefold structure arises from the need to prove that the standpoint from which one makes a transcendental argument must be internal to the argument itself. Fichte will claim that the contingent terms in an argument are reversible, highlighting the necessary internal relations that the juxtaposition of contingent terms always evokes. This can be summarized in the formula [1. (a + b); 2. (b + a); 3. their unity]. The result is that reason’s norms are found within the act of thinking: there is no discourse outside of reason.},
}

@article{chaput2025weltgeschichtweltgericht,
  author   = {Chaput, Emmanuel},
  title    = {{`Die Weltgeschichte ist das Weltgericht'}: {Hegel's} Early Critique of {Fichte's} Doctrine of Right ({Jena} 1801--1806)},
  journal  = {History of Political Thought},
  year     = {2025},
  volume   = {46},
  number   = {3},
  pages    = {501--526},
  doi      = {10.53765/20512988.46.3.501},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Chaput presents Hegel's early Jena critique of Fichte's political thought, arguing that Hegel's specifically empirical objections -- that Fichte's transcendental approach cannot escape formalism because it excludes the historical and empirical as legitimate judges of transcendental right -- are philosophically valid and indicative of Hegel's emerging standpoint in the Phenomenology. Hegel's claim is that insofar as Fichte commits to a systematic philosophy including a doctrine of right, the empirical and historical must be recognized as judicative instances.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an important external perspective on the systematic stakes of Fichte's position: Hegel's critique highlights precisely the tension between Fichte's a priori transcendental framework and the claim to accommodate genuine historical reality. This is a key comparative reference for understanding what is philosophically at issue in the domain's central questions.

  POSITION: External (Hegelian) critique, illuminating the limits of Fichte's transcendental approach to history from the standpoint of historical concreteness.
  },
  keywords = {hegel-critique-fichte, fichte-history, formalism, jena-berlin, Low},
  abstract = {In this paper, I present Hegel’s critique of Fichte’s political thought during the Jena period. Hegel’s general critique of Fichte has often been disregarded as unconvincing and unoriginal, but I argue that when one focuses on the specifically empirical aspects of
  abstract_source = {s2},
 Hegel’s critique, Fichte’s doctrine of right, one can actually see not only the validity of Hegel’s position, but its originality. I take it to be indicative of Hegel’s future approach to philosophy, and as such, as an important step in his intellectual development.
 Focusing on the specifically empirical dimension of Hegel’s critique could seem somewhat surprising since Fichte’s transcendental approach should be immune to such a non-transcendental critique. But I take Hegel’s point to be that insofar as Fichte commits to the ideal of
 a systematic philosophy which includes a doctrine of right, the empirical and historical should be understood as valid judicative instances for judging such a transcendental approach of right in order to avoid the accusation of formalism. In the end, I show how, according to Hegel, because
 of his transcendental presuppositions, Fichte can escape neither the accusation of formalism nor the judgment of history. This in turn points out the importance of Hegel’s critique as a manifestation of the early stages of what will soon be Hegel’s own philosophical position as
 presented in the Phenomenology of Spirit.},
}

@article{radrizzani2026jenawissenschaftslehre,
  author   = {Radrizzani, Ives},
  title    = {The {Jena} {Wissenschaftslehre} as an Answer to {Kant's} {Critique of the Power of Judgment}},
  journal  = {Open Philosophy},
  year     = {2026},
  volume   = {9},
  number   = {1},
  doi      = {10.1515/opphil-2025-0106},
  note     = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Radrizzani argues that the systematic development of the Wissenschaftslehre during the Jena period is driven by a continuous confrontation with Kant's third Critique. Fichte deemed Kant's solution to the gap between theoretical and practical philosophy insufficient; the theory of Aufforderung (solicitation), first developed in the Grundlage des Naturrechts, overcomes this gap, refutes Jacobi's charge of solipsism, and safeguards the unity of philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the systematic driving force behind the Jena Wissenschaftslehre's development -- the need to unify theoretical and practical philosophy -- which has direct bearing on understanding how history (as the domain of practical realization) fits within or strains against the a priori system. Relevant to understanding what the Jena system was trying to achieve before the Berlin transformation.

  POSITION: Continuity within the Jena period, emphasizing the practical-theoretical unification project as the key systematic concern.
  },
  keywords = {jena-wissenschaftslehre, kant-third-critique, systematic-development, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract This article demonstrates that the systematic progress in the elaboration of the Wissenschaftslehre during Fichte’s Jena period results from a continuous confrontation with Kant’s Critique of the Power of Judgment , which Fichte first read and reviewed in Leipzig. Fichte deems insufficient Kant’s proposed solution to bridge the “uncalculable” ( unübersehbare Kluft ) between theoretical and practical philosophy, a gap that jeopardizes philosophy as science. Only the theory of sollicitation ( Aufforderungstheorie ), first developed in the Foundations of Natural Right and later placed at the core of the New Presentation of the Wissenschaftslehre , allows for overcoming the unfinished nature of Kantian critique, refuting the accusation of solipsism raised by Jacobi, and safeguarding the unity of philosophy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{breazeale2013thinkingwissenschaftslehre,
  author    = {Breazeale, Daniel},
  title     = {Thinking Through the {Wissenschaftslehre}},
  year      = {2013},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi       = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199233632.001.0001},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A comprehensive critical study of Fichte's early (Jena) Wissenschaftslehre, addressing the relationship of the finite human self to the pure I, transcendental philosophy as 'pragmatic history of the mind,' the synthetic method, the Anstoss doctrine, and the primacy of the practical. Breazeale argues that the early Wissenschaftslehre harbors a distinctive account of the mind's self-constitution that requires both genetic (a priori) and pragmatic-historical dimensions.

  RELEVANCE: The concept of the Wissenschaftslehre as a 'pragmatic history of the mind' is directly pertinent to the domain's question about whether history has a systematic place in the early Fichte. Breazeale's treatment of the practical primacy and striving doctrine in the early system provides essential background for assessing continuity with the later philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Authoritative study of the Jena system; implicitly supports the view that historical-temporal structure is internal to the early Wissenschaftslehre.
  },
  keywords = {fichte-jena, wissenschaftslehre-method, pragmatic-history, striving, High},
  abstract = {Daniel Breazeale presents a critical study of the early philosophy of J.G. Fichte, and the version of the Wissenschaftslehre that Fichte developed between 1794 and 1799. He examines what Fichte was trying to accomplish and how he proposed to do so, and explores the difficulties implicit in his project and his strategies for overcoming them.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{beiser1987fate,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title = {The Fate of Reason: German Philosophy from Kant to Fichte},
  year = {1987},
  publisher = {Harvard University Press},
  doi = {10.4159/9780674020696},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser traces the philosophical development from Kant to Fichte through the intersection of rationalism, skepticism, and the reaction to Spinoza's pantheism in the 1780s-90s. The book reconstructs how post-Kantian thinkers, including Fichte, responded to the "crisis of reason" by radicalizing rather than moderating Kant's critical philosophy. A central claim is that Fichte's idealism emerges directly from this crisis as an attempt to provide systematic unity grounded in freedom.

  RELEVANCE: Essential background for understanding how Fichte situates himself in relation to Kant and why his philosophy of history takes the form it does: the attempt to ground historical development in the principle of freedom is a response to perceived failures in Kant's critical project. Beiser shows that the "from Kant to Hegel" narrative obscures the genuine philosophical debates that shaped Fichte.

  POSITION: Challenges the teleological reading of German Idealism as a seamless progression from Kant through Fichte to Hegel; supports the discontinuity thesis by showing Fichte's independent philosophical agenda.
  },
  keywords = {German-idealism, Fichte-Kant, philosophical-development, forerunner-debate, High},
  abstract = {Thanks to Beiser, we can newly appreciate the influence of Kant's critics on the development of his philosophy. Beiser brings the controversies, and the personalities who engaged in them, to life and tells a story that has uncanny parallels with the debates of the present.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{pinkard2002german,
  author = {Pinkard, Terry},
  title = {German Philosophy 1760--1860: The Legacy of Idealism},
  year = {2002},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511801846},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Pinkard offers a comprehensive narrative of German philosophy from the mid-18th century through mid-19th century, with Fichte, Schelling, and Hegel each treated as responding to the "Kantian paradox" of reason's self-authorization. On Fichte's philosophy of history, Pinkard emphasizes the transition from an individualistic ethics of self-determination toward the later nationalism and communal teleology of the Addresses and Characteristics.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the broadest scholarly narrative context for the forerunner debate, showing how the development of Hegel's historicism can be read as emerging from but also substantially transforming the Fichtean framework. Pinkard's treatment of the period 1794-1810 directly addresses the philosophical transformation relevant to assessing Fichte as forerunner.

  POSITION: Broadly continuationist -- the German Idealist tradition shows progressive philosophical development -- but attentive to Hegel's significant departures from Fichte.
  },
  keywords = {German-idealism, Fichte-Hegel, Pinkard, philosophical-narrative, High},
  abstract = {In the second half of the eighteenth century, German philosophy came for a while to dominate European philosophy. It changed the way in which not only Europeans, but people all over the world, conceived of themselves and thought about nature, religion, human history, politics, and the structure of the human mind. In this rich and wide-ranging book, Terry Pinkard interweaves the story of 'Germany' - changing during this period from a loose collection of principalities into a newly-emerged nation with a distinctive culture - with an examination of the currents and complexities of its developing philosophical thought. He examines the dominant influence of Kant, with his revolutionary emphasis on 'self-determination', and traces this influence through the development of romanticism and idealism to the critiques of post-Kantian thinkers such as Schopenhauer and Kierkegaard. His book will interest a range of readers in the history of philosophy, cultural history and the history of ideas.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{beiser2011historicist,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title = {The German Historicist Tradition},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199691555.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser reconstructs historicism as a distinct philosophical tradition, distinguishing between the German historicist movement (Herder, Ranke, Droysen, Dilthey) and the idealist philosophy of history associated with Kant and Hegel. The book argues that historicism is best understood as a reaction against Enlightenment universalism rather than as a variant of idealism, and provides a careful account of how Herder's particularist and pluralist approach to history differs from Kant's and Hegel's teleological universalism.

  RELEVANCE: Crucial for situating the forerunner debate: if Fichte is a "forerunner" of Hegel's historicism, Beiser's analysis raises the question of whether Fichte is better categorized as a historicist (in the Herderian sense) or as a transcendental teleologist (in the Kantian sense). The book challenges conflation of historicism with idealist philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Supports the view that historicism and idealist philosophy of history are distinct traditions, complicating simple forerunner narratives.
  },
  keywords = {historicism, Herder, Hegel, German-idealism, Beiser, High},
  abstract = {This is the first history in English of German historicism, the intellectual tradition which holds that history is the key to understanding all human values, beliefs and actions. Beiser surveys the key thinkers from the mid-18th to the early 20th century and illuminates the sources and reasons for this revolution in modern thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{beiser2019history,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick},
  title = {Hegel and the History of Idealism},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {28},
  number = {3},
  pages = {501--513},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2019.1661828},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser argues that the standard "Kant-Fichte-Schelling-Hegel" progression in the history of idealism is an artifact of Hegel's own self-serving historiography, which excluded romantic idealists (Holderlin, Novalis, Schlegel) and the opposing Herbartian tradition. The article calls for a broader conception of 19th-century idealism that does not treat Hegel as the culmination.

  RELEVANCE: Directly relevant to the forerunner debate: Beiser's argument undermines the narrative in which Fichte is merely a "step toward Hegel" by showing that this narrative was constructed by Hegel himself. This provides scholarly grounds for the discontinuity thesis and for treating Fichte's philosophy of history as having independent value.

  POSITION: Critical of forerunner narratives; defends a pluralist account of German Idealism.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, forerunner-debate, idealism-history, Beiser, discontinuity-thesis, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT This article attempts to expose an unwarranted narrowness in the study of idealism in nineteenth century philosophy, and to show that the field of idealism is much wider than usually assumed. This narrowness stems from the influence of Hegel’s history of philosophy, which saw the idealist tradition as beginning in Kant, passing through Fichte and Schelling, and then culminating in his own system. This conception of history has been disseminated by Hegel’s followers and still prevails today. I argue that this conception is too narrow for several reasons. First, it ignores the romantic idealists (Hölderlin, Novalis, Schlegel); second, it excludes the opposing tradition of idealism of Herbart, Fries and Beneke, which affirmed (against Schelling and Hegel) transcendental idealism, an empiricist epistemology, the existence of the thing-in-itself and the value of the Kantian dualisms; and, third, it neglects the history of idealism after Hegel later in the nineteenth century, more specifically, the idealism of Trendelenburg, Lotze and Hartmann.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{reichl2020kant,
  author = {Reichl, Pavel},
  title = {Kant's A Priori History of Metaphysics: Systematicity, Progress, and the Ends of Reason},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {29},
  number = {4},
  pages = {811--826},
  doi = {10.1111/ejop.12606},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reichl argues that Kant conceives of a priori history of metaphysics as a normatively structured narrative governed by the ends of reason, distinct from empirical historiography. The paper shows that Kant's account of rational progress in metaphysics serves as a model for subsequent idealist philosophies of history, including Fichte's, but that Kant's version is strictly regulative rather than constitutive.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the comparative framework for understanding what Fichte takes from Kant and where he departs when developing his own a priori philosophy of history. The Kantian model clarifies why Fichte's move from regulative to constitutive a priori in historical thinking marks a decisive step.

  POSITION: Supports the view of Fichte as transforming Kant's regulative approach into a more ambitious constitutive account, relevant to the forerunner debate.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, a-priori-history, systematicity, progress, Fichte-comparison, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper explores Kant's conception of the relation between philosophy and its history. The idea that philosophy must account for its historical development is often associated with German Idealism. On the traditional view, the German Idealists departed from the ahistorical Kantian framework by conceiving of reason in a developmental manner, thus initiating a “ historical turn ” within philosophy. How-ever, I argue that Kant's sketch of the history of metaphysics in the final chapter of the Critique of Pure Reason and in the so-called Progress Essay anticipates this turn. Specifically, I argue that in this essay Kant articulates a developmental conception of reason as a basis for understanding the history of metaphysics. My aim is to change the prevailing view of Kant as an ahistorical thinker and to challenge the canonical account of the genesis of the historical turn in philosophy. an a priori plan for organizing the body of knowledge of past philosophies. Second, this schema not be static, as on the Fülleborn model, but should be able to account for the succession of distinct historical},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{raysmith2025hegel,
  author = {Raysmith, Thomas},
  title = {Hegel and the Problem of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2025},
  publisher = {Bloomsbury Publishing Plc},
  doi = {10.5040/9781350423794},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Raysmith examines Hegel's justification for the claim that philosophy has a history, contrasting this with Kant's ahistorical conception. He reconstructs the "structure of exemplarity" -- a dynamic between universality, particularity, and singularity -- as central to Hegel's historical thinking, and provides a genetic account showing how this structure emerges through Fichte's and Schelling's intermediate positions.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question of whether Fichte anticipates Hegel's developmental historicism by showing that the transition from Kant to Hegel specifically involves Fichte's transformation of the concept of philosophical development. The detailed reconstruction of Fichte's intermediate position is directly relevant to the forerunner debate.

  POSITION: Interprets Fichte as an essential transitional figure between Kant's ahistorical and Hegel's fully historical conception of philosophy, supporting a nuanced continuity thesis.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, philosophy-of-history, Fichte-Kant, developmental-historicism, forerunner-debate, High},
  abstract = {Drawing on the work of major philosophers in 18th and 19th-century German idealism, Thomas Raysmith critically examines G. W. F. Hegel's justification for the claim that philosophy has a history.
  abstract_source = {s2},
 While Kant regarded philosophy as ahistorical, Hegel considered it to be a discipline that is necessarily historical, and elaborated a 'logical structure' that was supposed to allow it to have a history. Calling this structure, which Hegel took to be the fundamental structure of thought itself, 'the structure of exemplarity', Raysmith presents it as a dynamic reciprocity between universality, particularity and singularity. He provides a historical reconstruction of the shifting conceptions of philosophy from Kant, through J. G. Fichte and F. W. J. Schelling, to Hegel, and offers a systematic analysis of Hegel'sScience of Logicbased on a close, critical reading.
 Offering a compelling and novel reading of Hegel's thought,Hegel and the Problem of the History of Philosophyis a groundbreaking work for students and scholars of German idealism and the history of philosophy more broadly.},
}

@article{ferrer2018realismo,
  author = {Ferrer, Diogo},
  title = {Realismo e Reconcilia{\c c}{\~a}o na Filosofia da Hist{\'o}ria de Hegel},
  journal = {Revista Opini{\~a}o Filos{\'o}fica},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {8},
  number = {2},
  pages = {3--20},
  doi = {10.36592/opiniaofilosofica.v8i2.793},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ferrer argues that German classical philosophy promoted a "historical turn" of Kant's transcendental philosophy, with Fichte's theory of history -- grounded on a priori elements and the realization of freedom -- serving as a key basis for this turn. Hegel then accepts Fichte's framework of history as freedom's realization but reconceives philosophy of history as detecting the "thread of reason" in empirical facts rather than establishing a priori laws, enabling reconciliation with evil in history.

  RELEVANCE: Explicitly positions Fichte as the basis for Hegel's philosophy of history, supporting the continuity thesis while specifying the nature of Hegel's transformation. The argument that Hegel "accepts" but transforms Fichte's framework is directly relevant to the forerunner debate.

  POSITION: Continuationist -- Fichte's a priori history of freedom is the direct precursor to Hegel's reconciliation-oriented philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Fichte, philosophy-of-history, freedom, reconciliation, forerunner-debate, Medium},
  abstract = {German classical philosophy has promoted a ‘historical turn’ of Kant’s transcendental philosophy. One main basis for this movement is Fichte’s theory of history, which was grounded on a priori elements. Accepting Fichte’s understanding of history as the realization process of freedom, Hegel conceived the philosophy of history not as the establishment of laws for history, but as the detection of the thread of reason in the empirical facts. This article tries to show how Hegel, although accepting the massive presence of evil in history, conceives the possibility of a reconciliation with the facts of universal history, understood as the process of the transformation of power into recognition institutions.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{ferrer2024transformation,
  author = {Ferrer, Diogo},
  title = {The Transformation of Reason: Studies on System, Myth, and History in German Idealism},
  year = {2024},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004697836},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ferrer examines how Kant, Fichte, Schelling, and Hegel each transformed the concept of reason -- through skepticism and dialectics -- making possible the critical reversal of values characteristic of 20th-century philosophy. On history, the book argues that German Idealism represents a systematic engagement with myth, history, and system as three modes of articulating reason's self-understanding.

  RELEVANCE: The book's comparative approach across Fichte, Schelling, and Hegel on the question of history and system bears directly on how to understand Fichte's place in the tradition and his relation to Hegel's more fully historical-dialectical account.

  POSITION: Treats the transformation of reason as a collective achievement of the idealist tradition rather than a linear progression, complicating simple forerunner narratives.
  },
  keywords = {German-idealism, Kant, Fichte, Schelling, Hegel, reason-history, Medium},
  abstract = {Critique, skepticism, conflict, incompleteness, nothingness, irrational abyss, evil, and even genocide… That is what German idealism is also about. Trying to chart human reason as an architectural system, Kant, Fichte, Hegel, and Schelling uncovered that the most significant problems lie beneath the ground, in the foundations. Can reason survive the discovery of what lies at its depths? And should it? This book ventures into these foundations, addressing the keen philosophical innovations of German idealists. Through comparative and development studies, it presents fresh interpretations of how these leading thinkers reconstructed reason on unexplored territories. The greatest hazard was triggering an enduring inversion of values.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{oncina2019philosophy,
  author = {Oncina Coves, Faustino},
  title = {Philosophy of History and Politics in Fichte},
  booktitle = {A filosofia da hist{\'o}ria e da cultura em Fichte},
  year = {2019},
  pages = {13--40},
  publisher = {Imprensa da Universidade de Coimbra},
  doi = {10.14195/978-989-26-1754-1_1},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Oncina Coves examines the relationship between Fichte's philosophy of history and his political philosophy, arguing that the two are structurally linked through the concept of the vocation of the human species and the role of the state in historical development.

  RELEVANCE: Contributes to the assessment of whether Fichte's philosophy of history can be distinguished from political teleology, and whether its historicist elements are genuinely developmental or merely normative.

  POSITION: Interprets Fichte's historical thinking as embedded in a political-normative framework, with implications for how it relates to Hegel's more immanently historical approach.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, politics, philosophy-of-history, vocation, Medium},
  abstract = {The philosophy of history is so closely intertwined with the central
  abstract_source = {openalex},
concepts of modern politics that the campaign of discredit by the
conceptual historians (Koselleck and Marquard, for example) against
this young discipline, forged in the transition between the 18th and
the 19th centuries, has been based on pointing out the ideological
pathologies that it has caused (its totalitarian pretensions, or the
optimist and, ultimately, terrorist interpretation of world history). The
appeal to the classics can contribute to clarify the problems that harass
it nowadays, and also to somehow correct the distorted and even
parodic image that in general the conceptual historians presented of
the philosophy of history.
Fichte’s version is overwritten on the palimpsest of Kant’s, who
masterfully lays out the trending issues (ontological, epistemological
and narrative) in the philosophy of history, and inserts itself in
the double effort, prototypical of classical German philosophy, to
understand the historical world as a product of freedom, irreductible
to nature’s causality and to the constant regularity prevailing in the
physical world, and to ease the way for a historiography established on new bases in order to free itself of a disconsolate view of history
at the mercy of blind hazard. The amphibious character is a constant
in the philosophy of history (as also happens in the case of its twin
soul, politics), astride between the Wissenschaftslehre and the historical
experience. Thanks to that character, it formulates a critical diagnosis
of the past and the present and a prognosis concerning a future culture
full of reason and freedom.},
}

@incollection{roccollozano2019ancient,
  author = {Rocco Lozano, Valerio},
