  POSITION: Pluralistic volume representing apologetic, critical, and
  contextualist readings in dialogue.
  },
  keywords = {Addresses, nationalism, education, reception, collection, Medium}
}

@article{fuchs2010founder,
  author = {Fuchs, Erich},
  title = {Fichte the Founder of German Nationalism? Contribution to the Understanding of the Reden an die deutsche Nation},
  year = {2010},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fuchs argues that the image of Fichte as the founder of
  German nationalism is a historically constructed prejudice. The Reden
  were written by Fichte as an Enlightenment and cosmopolitan thinker for
  purposes of spiritual and political rebirth after the defeat of 1806,
  then put aside during the Restoration; only during the Bismarckian Reich
  were they reinterpreted on a nationalist key and transformed into the
  founding text of pan-German nationalism. The educational project of the
  Reden, Fuchs contends, can only be understood within the context of
  Fichte's overall transcendental system.

  RELEVANCE: Classic reception-history argument for the systematic distortion
  of Fichte's historical-political thought by nationalist appropriation.
  Identifies the specific historical moment (Bismarckian Reich) at which
  the ideological transformation occurred.

  POSITION: Apologetic-historical: defends Fichte's original intentions
  against the nationalist myth by periodizing the reception.

  INCOMPLETE
  },
  keywords = {reception-history, nationalism, Bismarck, Enlightenment, Reden, Low}
}

